for every irrational rotation]]
#align(center)[OpenAI]
#align(center)[September 27, 2026]

== 1 Introduction <sec:introduction>

Let $theta in \( 0 \, 1 \)$ be irrational. In the tracial representation let $R_theta$ be the von Neumann algebra
  generated by unitaries $U \, V$ with

$ V U = e^(2 pi i theta) U V \, wide tau \( U^m V^n \) = 0
  quad \( \( m \, n \) in bb(Z)^2 \\ { \( 0 \, 0 \) } \) \, wide tau \( 1 \) = 1 . $

This is the hyperfinite factor of type $upright("II")_1$;
  the concrete representation used below is the group-measure-space representation of irrational rotation.
  For a continuous function $f$ on the unit circle $bb(T)$, write $T_f = U f \( V \)$.
  A closed invariant subspace is #emph[affiliated with $R_theta$] when its orthogonal projection belongs to
  $R_theta$. A nontrivial such subspace is therefore equivalent to a projection $p in R_theta$ satisfying

$ 0 < tau \( p \) < 1 \, wide \( 1 - p \) T_f p = 0 . $

There is no requirement that $p$ commute with $T_f$.

Let $m$ denote normalized Haar measure on $bb(T)$. The Fuglede–Kadison determinant of the scalar weight is

$ Delta \( f \( V \) \) = exp (integral_(bb(T)) log \| f \( z \) \| thin d m \( z \)) \, $

with $log 0 = - oo$ and $exp \( - oo \) = 0$. Zhu, Fang, and Shi asked whether $T_f$ must have a nontrivial
  affiliated invariant subspace if $f$ is nonzero almost everywhere, $\| f \|$ is nonconstant,
  and this determinant is zero #cite(label("zfs")) p. 264, Question after Proposition 5.7.
  We answer their question negatively for every prescribed irrational angle.

#thm([Theorem 1.1.])[
  #emph[For every irrational $theta in \( 0 \, 1 \)$ there exists a continuous function $f :
    bb(T) arrow.r \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ such that
$ f^(- 1) \( { 0 } \) = { 1 } \, wide integral_(bb(T)) log f thin d m = - oo \, $

 and every projection $p in R_theta$ satisfying $\( 1 - p \) U f \( V \) p = 0$ is either $0$ or $1$.]
] <thm:main>

The angle is fixed before the weight is constructed; no Diophantine condition is imposed.
  The conclusion concerns projections in the specified containing factor.
  Arbitrary invariant subspaces of its Hilbert-space representation need not have their projections in that
  factor. Corollary 22 also shows directly that the constructed operator is nonzero and norm-quasinilpotent.

=== 1.1 Hyperinvariant subspaces and commutants <hyperinvariant-subspaces-and-commutants>

For a bounded operator $T$ on a complex Hilbert space $cal(H)$, write ${ T }' = { A in B \( cal(H) \) : A T =
  T A }$. A closed subspace is #emph[hyperinvariant] for $T$ if every member of ${ T }'$ leaves it invariant.
  An operator algebra is #emph[transitive] if its only common closed invariant subspaces are $0$ and $cal(H)$.

#thm([Lemma 1.2.])[
  (Commutant algebra).  #emph[If $T in B \( cal(H) \)$ is nonscalar, then ${ T }'$ is a proper,
    strong-operator closed, unital complex algebra. It is transitive exactly when $T$ has no nontrivial
    hyperinvariant subspace.]
] <lem:commutant-algebra>

#proof[
  The commutation equation is preserved by addition, multiplication and scalar multiplication,
    and the identity commutes with $T$. If $A_lambda arrow.r A$ strongly and $A_lambda T = T A_lambda$,
    then for every $zeta in cal(H)$ both sides converge, giving $A T zeta = T A zeta$.
    Thus the algebra is strongly closed. If every bounded operator commuted with $T$,
    then every rank-one orthogonal projection would commute with $T$,
    so every nonzero vector would be an eigenvector. Applying this to $x \, y \, x +
    y$ for linearly independent vectors shows that the eigenvalues are equal; hence $T$ would be scalar.
    Properness follows. The last assertion is precisely the two definitions. ◻
]

The bridge to the containing von Neumann algebra is the elementary observation recorded in
  #cite(label("dykema2005"), form: "prose") Proposition 2.1.

#thm([Lemma 1.3.])[
  #emph[Let $cal(M) subset B \( cal(H) \)$ be a von Neumann algebra and $T in cal(M)$.
    The orthogonal projection onto any hyperinvariant subspace for $T$ belongs to $cal(M)$ and is invariant for $T$.]
] <lem:hyperinvariant-projection>

#proof[
  Every unitary $W in cal(M)'$ and its inverse commute with $T$.
    Hyperinvariance therefore makes the subspace reducing for $W$.
    Its orthogonal projection commutes with all such unitaries and hence belongs to $cal(M)'' = cal(M)$.
    Since $T in { T }'$, its range is invariant under $T$. ◻
]

#thm([Corollary 1.4.])[
  #emph[Every separable infinite-dimensional complex Hilbert space carries a nonzero norm-quasinilpotent operator
    with no nontrivial hyperinvariant subspace. Its commutant is a proper strongly closed transitive unital algebra.]
] <cor:transitive-commutant>

#proof[
  The tracial representation of $R_theta$ is separable and infinite-dimensional.
    Apply Theorem 1, Corollary 22, and Lemma 3 there. A nonzero quasinilpotent operator is nonscalar,
    so Lemma 2 applies. Unitary equivalence transfers these properties to any separable infinite-dimensional
    complex Hilbert space. ◻
]

These conclusions do not exclude ordinary invariant subspaces in the representation.
  A separate companion gives a direct $2$-adic weighted shift with a transitive commutant,
  including an explicit norm estimate, backward intertwiners and a description of its polar conjugates
  #cite(label("direct-companion")). It uses only Lemmas 2 and 9 from this paper.
  The present proof uses no result from that companion.

#prodef([Remark 1.5.])[
  Corollary 4 contradicts the hyperinvariant-subspace assertion of #cite(label("cho-ko-lee"), form: "prose")
    Corollary 2.8, p. 801 at $mu = 1$: for the nonzero quasinilpotent $T$ just constructed and any conjugation
    $C$, their stated hypotheses apply to $I + T$, but ${ I + T }' = { T }'$, so $I +
    T$ has no nontrivial hyperinvariant subspace. Only that hyperinvariant-subspace assertion is at issue here.
] <rem:ckl>

=== 1.2 Prior work <prior-work>

The determinant of Fuglede and Kadison #cite(label("fuglede-kadison")) and Brown’s spectral distribution
  #cite(label("brown")) form a major route to invariant subspaces in finite factors.
  Haagerup and Schultz associate an affiliated hyperinvariant projection to each Borel subset of the plane,
  with trace equal to its Brown mass #cite(label("haagerup-schultz")) Theorem 1.1.
  Thus a Brown measure that is not a point mass gives a nontrivial projection.
  For a point-mass Brown measure, the trace formula gives only trivial projections.

Point-mass Brown measure can nevertheless coexist with many invariant subspaces.
  Dykema and Haagerup construct a one-parameter family of nontrivial affiliated hyperinvariant subspaces for the
  quasinilpotent DT-operator #cite(label("dykema-haagerup")) Theorems 2.2 and 2.9.
  Thus degeneration of Brown measure to a point mass does not itself exclude invariant projections.
  Tucci also constructed quasinilpotent generators of the hyperfinite factor with nontrivial affiliated invariant
  projections #cite(label("Tucci2008")) Theorems 3.1 and 3.3 and the discussion following Theorem 3.3.
  These examples further distinguish quasinilpotence from the absence of factor-invariant projections.

For weighted irrational rotations, the measurable-action theorem of Dykema and Schultz identifies the Brown
  measure as uniform measure on the circle whose radius is the determinant #cite(label("dykema-schultz"))
  Theorem 5.4. Zhu, Fang, and Shi give a direct proof, explicitly crediting that earlier result
  #cite(label("zfs")) Theorem 5.3. At determinant zero this measure is the point mass at zero,
  so the Brown-measure projections are trivial.

Continuous weights with one zero and determinant zero were already exhibited by Zhu, Fang,
  and Shi #cite(label("zfs")) pp. 262–263. The additional conclusion of Theorem 1 is the exclusion of every
  nontrivial invariant projection for a suitable weight at each prescribed angle.
  Zhu, Fang, and Shi’s spectral-radius formula #cite(label("zfs")) Theorem 2.3 implies quasinilpotence in this
  case. We give an elementary proof of this special case, using only uniform averaging of continuous functions
  under irrational rotation. Their positive-determinant invariant-subspace result #cite(label("zfs"))
  Corollary 5.5 has a hypothesis complementary to the one studied here.

=== 1.3 Proof strategy <proof-strategy>

The obstruction begins with a measurable weight. Suppose its weighted rotation had a nontrivial invariant
  projection. In the shift representation, this projection becomes a field of projections.
  The finite trace and ergodicity force its central diagonal entry to lie strictly between zero and one almost
  everywhere. Thus both the projection and its complement have vectors with a nonzero central coordinate.

Invariance imposes weighted orthogonality identities on these vectors.
  We first obtain the identities along rotation orbits and then pass them to suitably coupled pairs of points.
  The weight has drops that are rare enough to leave a chosen finite window under control,
  but strong enough to suppress contributions outside that window.
  The central coordinates force at least one finite convolution coefficient to stay away from zero.
  The suppressed tail cannot cancel it, contradicting the exact orthogonality identity.
  Conditioning separately on each endpoint controls that tail even though it has infinitely many terms.

The measurable weight is then replaced by a continuous weight with one zero.
  Smoothing and moving the logarithmic drops changes their sum by a constant plus a measurable coboundary.
  This relation supplies an intertwining multiplier, possibly unbounded.
  A domain and trace argument shows that an invariant projection for the continuous operator would produce one for
  the measurable operator with exactly the same trace. This transfers the obstruction to the desired continuous weight.

The construction realizing this scheme is as follows. Use the additive circle $X = bb(R) \/ bb(Z)$,
  with Haar probability $mu$, and write $sigma x = x + theta$. Write ${ t } in \[ 0 \, 1 \)$ for fractional part.
  We construct divisible integers $1 = q_0 divides q_1 divides q_2 divides dots.h.c$ and positive costs $c_i$.
  The measurable model is the weight

$ g_(*) \( x \) = sum_(i gt.eq 1) c_i floor.l { q_i x } + { q_i theta } floor.r \, wide f_(*) \( x \) =
  e^(- g_(*) \( x \)) . $

The series is finite almost everywhere although its integral diverges.
  Large divisible frequencies allow its trial phases to be realized at the fixed angle $theta$,
  while finitely many sampling tests approximate independent Haar coordinates.
  The circle characters themselves are dependent. A mixed-radix expansion supplies independent digits,
  from which we build the ordered couplings with controlled marginal densities.
  The prescribed-marginal viewpoint belongs to the classical coupling theory of Strassen and Edwards
  #cite(label("Strassen1965")) #cite(label("Edwards1978")); here we also need approximation by positive orbit pairs.

Section 2 fixes the frequencies and sampling powers. Sections 3 and 4 obtain the projection identities on the
  ordered pairs. Section 5 proves and applies the convolution obstruction,
  and Section 6 constructs the continuous weight and proves the trace-preserving transfer.

Appendix 7 gives a complete alternative realization on an infinite product of circles.
  Independent coordinates yield a finite-coordinate orbit-sampling theorem and a second application of the same
  convolution obstruction. That model also separates the atomic algebras generated by finitely many polar
  conjugates from the diffuse algebra generated by the full orbit.
  The prescribed-angle circle proof is complete before the appendix.

All identities involving measurable fields hold on appropriate conull sets.
  When integer iterates are used, we intersect the countably many translates of these sets.
  The constants implicit in $O \( dot.op \)$ below are absolute unless a fixed construction stage is specified.
  Every new ingredient needed for the proof is established in the paper.

== 2 Sampling at a prescribed irrational angle <sec:parameters>

Fix $X = bb(R) \/ bb(Z)$ with Haar probability $mu$, and put $sigma x = x + theta$,
  where $theta in \( 0 \, 1 \)$ is a fixed irrational number.
  We write ${ t } in \[ 0 \, 1 \)$ for fractional part. Our immediate aim is to construct rare logarithmic drops
  whose characters behave almost like independent coordinates in finitely many sampling tests.
  The frequencies will be divisible integers; the angle $theta$ will never be changed.

We use the following elementary facts about irrational rotations.
  For each nonzero integer $a$, the average of $e^(2 pi i a n theta)$ over $1 lt.eq n lt.eq N$ tends to zero,
  by the formula for a geometric sum. Trigonometric approximation gives equidistribution against continuous
  functions, and continuous upper and lower bounds give it for intervals.
  The same argument gives convergence uniform in the starting point for continuous functions.
  In particular, every positive orbit is dense. These facts also hold with $theta$ replaced by any positive
  integer multiple of $theta$.

For an integer $m gt.eq 200$, put $h = m^(- 3 \/ 5)$ and $Delta \( z \) = min \( { z } \, 1 - { z } \)$.
  Define on two circles

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.1)")
 $ w_m \( z \, v \) = upright(bold(1))_({ Delta \( z \) gt.eq m^(- 2) })
   frac(upright(bold(1))_({ 0 < v < h Delta \( z \) }), h Delta \( z \)) \, wide w_m^(\( 1 \)) \( z \, v \) =
   w_m \( z \, v \) \, quad w_m^(\( 2 \)) \( z \, v \) = w_m \( z - v \, v \) \, $ <eq:sampling-kernel>]

where $v$ is represented in $\[ 0 \, 1 \)$ and the expression is zero when the cutoff fails.
  The first kernel forces a small positive move with no wrap around zero.
  The second is its density viewed from the endpoint.

The kernels have almost unit mass at either endpoint. We next control their second moments and the correlations
  produced by two separated integer powers. These estimates will let us average products over a whole block of
  coordinates. The scalar lemmas also serve the independent-coordinate construction in Appendix 7.

#thm([Lemma 2.1.])[
  (Scalar sampling estimates).  #emph[Let $m gt.eq 200$ be an integer, put $h = m^(- 3 \/ 5)$, and let $u =
    w_m^(\( r \))$ for either $r = 1$ or $r = 2$. Write $macron(u) \( s \) =
    integral_0^1 u \( s \, v \) thin d v$. Uniformly in $s$, the supremum plus total variation of $v mapsto
    u \( s \, v \)$ is $O \( m^(13 \/ 5) \)$, hence $O \( m^10 \)$, and
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.2)")
 $ & parallel macron(u) parallel_oo lt.eq 2 \, wide integral_0^1 macron(u) = 1 - 2 m^(- 2) \,\
 & \( 1 - 2 m^(- 2) \)^2 lt.eq integral_0^1 macron(u)^2 lt.eq 1 + frac(h^2, \( 1 - h \)^2) \, wide
   integral.double_(\[ 0 \, 1 \]^2) u^2 = 2 / h log m^2 / 2 . $ <eq:kernel-estimates>]

]
  #emph[For the sampling estimate let $0 < N_0 lt.eq N lt.eq m^2$, $Q = m^(\( 7 \/ 10 \) m)$ and $H = m^300$.
    Let $lambda$ be uniform on $\[ 1 \/ N_0 \, 2 \/ N_0 \]$. If $n in \[ Q \/ 2 \, Q \] ∩ bb(N)$,
    the density $p_n$ of ${ n lambda }$ satisfies
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.3)")
 $ \| p_n - 1 \| lt.eq N_0 \/ n quad upright("almost everywhere") . $ <eq:scalar-marginal>]

 If two distinct such powers $n \, n'$ satisfy
$ \| v n +
  v' n' \| > N m^1000 quad \( 0 < \| v \| + \| v' \| \, med \| v \| \, \| v' \| lt.eq H \, med v \, v' in bb(Z) \) \, $

 then, for intervals $I \, J subset \[ 0 \, 1 \)$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.4)")
 $ lr(|bb(P) \( { n lambda } in I \, { n' lambda } in J \) - \| I \| \| J \||) =
   O \( m^(- 80) \) . $ <eq:scalar-rectangles>]

 Consequently, for complex functions $f \, g$ of bounded variation on $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$, with $B \( f \) =
   parallel f parallel_oo + "Var" \( f \)$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.5)")
 $ lr(|bb(E) \[ f \( { n lambda } \) g \( { n' lambda } \) \] - (integral_0^1 f) (integral_0^1 g)|) lt.eq
   C m^(- 80) B \( f \) B \( g \) . $ <eq:scalar-bv>]

 All implicit constants are absolute, for sufficiently large $m$, and uniform in $N_0 \, N \, n \,
   n'$ and the intervals or functions.]
] <lem:scalar-sampling-estimates>

#proof[
  For the first orientation the function of $v$ is a constant height at most $m^(13 \/ 5)$ on an interval,
    so the variation bound is immediate. For the second, nonzero values require $v < s$ in representatives $s \,
    v in \[ 0 \, 1 \)$: if $v > s$, then $Delta \( s - v \) lt.eq v - s < v$, whereas $h < 1$.
    On $0 < v < s$, the conditions $Delta \( s - v \) gt.eq m^(- 2)$ and $v < h
    Delta \( s - v \)$ describe an interval. The reciprocal of $Delta \( s - v \)$ has at most two monotone pieces
    there, with the same height bound. This proves the asserted uniform bound in both orientations.
  In the first orientation $macron(u)$ is the cutoff indicator, and
  $ integral.double u^2 = 2 / h integral_(m^(- 2))^(1 \/ 2) frac(d s, s) = 2 / h log \( m^2 \/ 2 \) . $
  The change $\( s \, v \) mapsto \( s - v \, v \)$ preserves double integrals,
    giving the same total mass and square integral for the second orientation.
    To bound its averaged density, first omit the cutoff. The resulting density $d$ is the law of $z +
    t Delta \( z \)$ for independent uniform $z in \[ 0 \, 1 \)$ and $t in \( 0 \, h \)$.
    For fixed $t$ this is an increasing piecewise-linear bijection of $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$, with slopes $1 + t$ and $1 -
    t$. Thus $integral d = 1$ and $\| d - 1 \| lt.eq h \/ \( 1 - h \)$, whence
  $ integral d^2 = 1 + integral \( d - 1 \)^2 lt.eq 1 + frac(h^2, \( 1 - h \)^2) . $
  Restoring the cutoff only decreases this nonnegative density; also $d lt.eq \( 1 - h \)^(- 1) lt.eq 2$.
    These observations give the upper bounds in (2.2), while Cauchy–Schwarz gives the lower bound from its exact
    mass. The cancellation of the linear term here is essential when multiplying estimates over $m$ coordinates.
  Periodizing a uniform interval of length $n \/ N_0$ gives (2.3); in particular $p_n lt.eq 2$ for large $m$.
    For the separated powers and any permitted nonzero frequency pair,
    direct integration on $\[ 1 \/ N_0 \, 2 \/ N_0 \]$ gives
  $ lr(|bb(E) e^(2 pi i \( v n + v' n' \) lambda)|) lt.eq frac(N_0, pi \| v n + v' n' \|) lt.eq 1 / pi m^(- 1000) . $
  To pass from characters to rectangles, convolve each interval indicator with the Fejér kernel
  $ F_H \( s \) = 1 / H (frac(sin \( pi H s \), sin \( pi s \)))^2 . $
  Expanding the squared geometric sum shows that $F_H$ is nonnegative, has mass one,
    and has frequencies of magnitude below $H$. The convolved indicators lie in $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$,
    preserve their means, and have Fourier coefficients of modulus at most one.
    Outside strips of width $eta = m^(- 80)$ about interval endpoints, their error is $O \( \( H eta \)^(- 1) \)$,
    by integrating the kernel tail. The marginal bound $p_n \, p_(n') lt.eq
    2$ shows that replacing both indicators in a product costs $O \( eta + \( H eta \)^(- 1) \)$,
    both for the actual joint law and for Haar product measure.
    The remaining Fourier error is $O \( H^2 m^(- 1000) \)$. This proves (2.4); no joint density,
    or independence of the sampled powers, is used.
  Finally, a bounded-variation function is, up to a null set,
    a constant plus an integral of indicators of intervals $\[ a \, 1 \)$ against its complex Stieltjes measure.
    The constant and measure have total size at most $B \( f \)$; the same holds for $g$.
    Apply (2.4) to the resulting products of interval indicators, allowing the full interval.
    Integration against total variation gives (2.5). Changes at the countably many possible discontinuities do not
    matter, because the marginals have densities. ◻
]

Before choosing the block scales, we record the finite-coefficient bound that will determine how much attenuation
  each block must supply.

#thm([Lemma 2.2.])[
  (A nonzero finite convolution).  #emph[For every integer $K gt.eq 0$ and $D gt.eq delta > 0$ there is $epsilon =
    epsilon \( K \, D \, delta \) > 0$ such that complex Laurent polynomials $A \,
    B$ supported in $\[ - K \, K \]$, with coefficients of modulus at most $D$ and $\| A_0 \| \, \| B_0 \| gt.eq
    delta$, satisfy
$ max_(0 lt.eq n lt.eq 2 K) \| \( A B \)_(- n) \| gt.eq epsilon . $

]
] <lem:polynomial-coefficient>

#proof[
  The admissible polynomial pairs form a nonempty compact set.
    For each pair, the first nonzero exponents of $A$ and $B$ are at most zero,
    and their sum belongs to $\[ - 2 K \, 0 \]$. The corresponding product coefficient is nonzero.
    The continuous displayed maximum therefore has a positive minimum on the compact set. ◻
]

We now choose the parameters for the fixed circle rotation. First prepare data for the later continuity argument.
  Let $O_j$ be the open arc of radius $1 \/ \( 10 j \)$ about zero.
  Choose a continuous nonnegative bump supported in $O_j$ and positive on a smaller arc.
  Finitely many of its translates $x mapsto psi \( sigma^n x \)$, with $0 lt.eq n lt.eq h_j^(*)$,
  have a strictly positive sum on $X$, by density and compactness.
  Normalize by this sum to obtain continuous functions $phi.alt_(j \, n) gt.eq 0$ such that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.6)")
 $ sum_(n = 0)^(h_j^(*)) phi.alt_(j \, n) = 1 \, wide sigma^n \( "supp" phi.alt_(j \, n) \) subset O_j .
   $ <eq:partition>]

All $h_j^(*)$ and $phi.alt_(j \, n)$ are fixed before the construction below.

The parameters below are grouped into consecutive blocks of $m_j$ coordinates.
  In block $j$, $K_j$ is the cutoff for a finite convolution, $C_j$ is the cost of each logarithmic drop,
  and $L_j$ is the attenuation needed to make the omitted tail smaller than a finite polynomial coefficient.
  The total phase increment in the block is of order $1 \/ K_j$.
  The sampling horizon $Q_j$ lies well below the next cutoff $K_(j + 1)$,
  so sampling moves can be controlled in every future block.
  The two widths $a_j \, b_j$ will provide crossings in complementary ranges of the tail index.
  The proposition states these requirements together; its proof shows that they can all be chosen successively.

#thm([Proposition 2.3.])[
  #emph[For the fixed $theta$ there are positive integers $m_j \, K_j \, G_j \, q_i$,
    positive real numbers $L_j \, C_j \, epsilon_j$, and consecutive blocks $J_j = { d_(j - 1) + 1 \, dots.h \,
    d_j }$ of size $m_j$, with $d_0 = 0$, having the following properties. Set
$ q_0 = 1 \, wide u_i \( x \) = { q_i x } \, wide alpha_i = { q_i theta } \,\
kappa_j = \( 100 j \)^(- 1) \, wide N_j = m_j K_j \,\
a_j = G_j \/ m_j \, wide b_j = m_j^(- 19 \/ 20) \, wide Q_j = m_j^(\( 7 \/ 10 \) m_j) . $

 The frequencies are strictly increasing and divisible, and
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.7)")
 $ 1 / N_j < alpha_i < 2 / N_j \, wide q_i divides q_(i + 1) \, wide q_(i + 1) / q_i gt.eq
   N_j quad \( i in J_j \) . $ <eq:phases>]

 For every integer $k$ with $N_j \/ 4 lt.eq \| k \| lt.eq m_j^(\( 4 \/ 5 \) m_j)$,
