 Put $D_j = exp \( \( K_j + 1 \) sum_(s < j) C_s m_s \)$. If $A \,
   B$ are Laurent polynomials supported in $\[ - K_j \, K_j \]$, every coefficient has modulus at most $D_j$,
   and $\| A_0 \| \, \| B_0 \| gt.eq 1 \/ j$, then
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.10)")
 $ max_(0 lt.eq n lt.eq 2 K_j) \| \( A B \)_(- n) \| gt.eq epsilon_j \,
   wide \( 2 K_j + 1 \) 3 e^(- L_j \/ 10000) lt.eq epsilon_j \/ j . $ <eq:polynomial>]

]
  #emph[Finally put $p_(*) = d_(j - 1)$ and $ell_j = \( \( j + 1 \)^2 \( 1 + p_(*) \) q_(p_(*)) \)^(- 1)$.
    For every $t = r \/ q_(p_(*))$ with $r in bb(Z)$, $0 lt.eq r < q_(p_(*))$,
    there is a finite nonempty set $cal(N)_(j \, t)$ of positive integers in $\[ Q_j \/ 2 \, Q_j \]$,
    with ${ n theta - t } in \( 0 \, ell_j \)$, such that for $r' = 1 \, 2$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.11)")
 $ ∥frac(1, \| cal(N)_(j \, t) \|) sum_(n in cal(N)_(j \, t)) product_(i in J_j)
   w_(m_j)^(\( r' \)) \( u_i \( x \) \, { n alpha_i } \) - 1∥_(L^2 \( mu \)) < 1 / j . $ <eq:sampling>]

]
] <prop:parameters>

The proof separates two choices. We first choose independent trial phases to obtain the finite probabilistic
  tests. Only afterwards do we realize them, to sufficient accuracy, by characters of the prescribed rotation.
  Large gaps between successive frequencies also make those characters converge in distribution to independent
  Haar variables.

#proof[
