Two consequences of the construction will be used repeatedly:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.14)")
 $ sum_(s > j) 1 / K_s lt.eq 2 / K_(j + 1) \, wide sum_j frac(1 + h_j^(*), K_j) < oo \, wide Q_j /
   K_(j + 1) arrow.r 0 . $ <eq:tail-sizes>]

They follow respectively from $K_(j + 1) > 2 K_j$, the final inequality in (2.9), and $7 \/ 10 < 3 \/ 4$.
  The parameters are now fixed independently of any putative invariant projection.
  We next turn their sampling identities into operator identities between pairs of points of the circle.

== 3 Projection fields and transfer between endpoints <sec:fields>

We now turn the parameters of Proposition 8 into a weighted shift.
  The purpose of this section is to convert invariance under that shift into an identity between projection fields
  at two different points of the circle. We first obtain the identity along positive rotation orbits,
  and then use the sampling estimates to reach every translation by an integer multiple of $1 \/ q_A$,
  for some $A gt.eq 1$.

=== 3.1 Fields determined by scalar multipliers <fields-determined-by-scalar-multipliers>

We first isolate the elementary field representation used here and in the direct weighted-shift construction.
  It depends only on commutation with scalar multipliers.

#thm([Lemma 3.1.])[
  #emph[Let $\( Y \, nu \)$ be a probability space, let $K = ell^2 \( bb(Z) \)$,
    and let $A in B \( L^2 \( Y \, nu \; K \) \)$ commute with every scalar multiplication operator $M_g
    zeta \( y \) = g \( y \) zeta \( y \)$, $g in L^oo \( Y \, nu \)$.
    There is a field $A \( y \) in B \( K \)$, unique almost everywhere, such that
$ \( A zeta \) \( y \) = A \( y \) zeta \( y \) \, wide parallel A \( y \) parallel lt.eq parallel A parallel quad
  upright("almost everywhere") . $

 The field is measurable in the sense that $y mapsto A \( y \) v$ is measurable for every $v in K$.
   Products and adjoints of such operators are computed fiberwise.
   In particular, an orthogonal projection is represented by orthogonal projections outside one null set.]
] <lem:scalar-multiplier-fields>

#proof[
  If $B in B \( L^2 \( Y \, nu \) \)$ commutes with all multipliers, put $b = B 1$. For every measurable set $E$,
  $ B upright(bold(1))_E = upright(bold(1))_E b \, wide integral_E \| b \|^2 thin d nu lt.eq
    parallel B parallel^2 nu \( E \) . $
  Thus $\| b \| lt.eq parallel B parallel$ almost everywhere.
    The identity $B f = b f$ follows first for simple functions and then, by density, for all $f in L^2 \( Y \)$.
    Apply this observation to the matrix entries of $A$ between the coordinate copies of $L^2 \( Y \)$,
    obtaining measurable functions $a_(i j)$.
  Let $v$ range over the countable set of finitely supported vectors with rational real and imaginary parts.
    Commutation with $M_(upright(bold(1))_E)$ and the operator bound give
  $ integral_E sum_(i in bb(Z)) lr(|sum_j a_(i j) \( y \) v_j|)^2 d nu \( y \) lt.eq
    parallel A parallel^2 parallel v parallel^2 nu \( E \) . $
  Consequently the integrand is at most $parallel A parallel^2 parallel v parallel^2$ almost everywhere.
    Outside the union of these countably many null sets, the matrix defines an operator $A \( y \)$ on $K$ with
    norm at most $parallel A parallel$. Its columns, and then its values on every vector,
    are measurable by finite-coordinate approximation. The represented field agrees with $A$ on finite-coordinate
    simple functions, hence on all of $L^2 \( Y \; K \)$.
  Equality of two such fields follows by applying their represented operators to each constant basis field.
    Products act fiberwise by the representation formula, and the entries of the adjoint field are
    $overline(a_(j i))$. This proves the assertions about products and adjoints.
    The identities $p = p^(*) = p^2$ then give orthogonal projections on a common conull set. ◻
]

=== 3.2 The tracial representation <the-tracial-representation>

We use the standard crossed-product representation for a probability-preserving action;
  see #cite(label("AnantharamanPopa"), form: "prose") Section 1.4 and Proposition 1.4.5.
  We verify below the field and trace properties needed in the proof.

Write $X = bb(R) \/ bb(Z)$, let $mu$ be Haar probability measure, and put $sigma x = x + theta$.
  On $cal(H) = L^2 \( X \, mu \; ell^2 \( bb(Z) \) \)$, let $S e_k = e_(k + 1)$ and define

$ U \( x \) = S \, wide pi \( a \) \( x \) e_k = a \( sigma^k x \) e_k quad \( a in L^oo \( X \, mu \) \) . $

For $V = pi \( x mapsto e^(2 pi i x) \)$, these operators satisfy $V U = e^(2 pi i theta) U V$.
  We also write $e_0$ for the constant field with this value.
  The vector $U^m V^n e_0$ is the field $x mapsto e^(2 pi i n x) e_m$.
  These vectors span a dense subspace by ordinary Fourier theory, and $chevron.l U^m V^n e_0 \,
  e_0 chevron.r$ equals $1$ for $\( m \, n \) = \( 0 \, 0 \)$ and $0$ otherwise.
  The commutation relation reduces every product of monomials to a scalar multiple of a monomial,
  so these moments identify all inner products of polynomial vectors with the prescribed tracial cyclic
  representation. The resulting unitary identifies the generated algebra $M$ with $R_theta$ and its faithful
  normal trace with $tau$. Measurable functional calculus for $V$ places every $pi \( a \)$ in $M$.

Every generator commutes with scalar multiplication by a function of $x$.
  Lemma 9 therefore represents every $A in M$ by an essentially bounded measurable field.
  The generators also commute with the unitary $J$ given by $\( J zeta \) \( x \) = S zeta \( sigma x \)$:
  for $pi \( a \)$ the coordinate calculation is $a \( sigma^(k - 1) \( sigma x \) \) = a \( sigma^k x \)$.
  These commutations pass to their von Neumann algebra. Testing $A J = J A$ on the constant basis fields gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.1)")
 $ A \( sigma x \) = S^(- 1) A \( x \) S quad upright("for almost every ") x . $ <eq:field-covariance>]

The vector formula for the trace is now

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.2)")
 $ tau \( A \) = integral_X chevron.l A \( x \) e_0 \, e_0 chevron.r thin d mu \( x \) wide \( A in M \) .
   $ <eq:field-trace>]

We choose Borel versions for projection fields and discard null sets and their countably many rotation translates
  when necessary. Irrational rotation is ergodic: all Fourier coefficients of nonzero index vanish for an
  invariant $L^2$ function. We use equidistribution in the form established at the beginning of Section 2.

The following consequence of covariance will also apply to the product model.
  It ensures that a nontrivial invariant projection has a nonzero zeroth coordinate at almost every endpoint,
  as does its complement.

#thm([Lemma 3.2.])[
  #emph[Let $sigma$ be an invertible ergodic probability-preserving transformation of $\( Y \, nu \)$.
    On $L^2 \( Y \, nu \; ell^2 \( bb(Z) \) \)$, define $\( U zeta \) \( y \) =
    S zeta \( y \)$ and $pi \( a \) \( y \) e_k = a \( sigma^k y \) e_k$, where $S e_k = e_(k + 1)$.
    Suppose a von Neumann algebra $M$ containing $U$ and $pi \( L^oo \( Y \) \)$ consists of measurable fields
    with covariance $A \( sigma y \) = S^(- 1) A \( y \) S$ and has faithful normal tracial state
$ tau \( A \) = integral_Y chevron.l A \( y \) e_0 \, e_0 chevron.r thin d nu \( y \) . $

 Let $f in L^oo \( Y \, nu \)$ be positive almost everywhere, put $T = U pi \( f \)$,
   and let $p in M$ be an orthogonal projection with $\( 1 - p \) T p = 0$.
   If $0 < tau \( p \) < 1$, then $0 < chevron.l p \( y \) e_0 \, e_0 chevron.r < 1$ almost everywhere.]
] <lem:positive-diagonal>

#proof[
  Put $r \( y \) = chevron.l p \( y \) e_0 \, e_0 chevron.r$ and $D_y = "diag"_k f \( sigma^k y \)$.
    Invariance and covariance give $D_y "ran" p \( y \) subset.eq "ran" p \( sigma y \)$.
    If $r \( sigma y \) = 0$, every vector in the latter range has zero zeroth coordinate.
    Since $\( D_y v \)_0 = f \( y \) v_0$ and $f \( y \) > 0$,
    every vector in the former range also has zero zeroth coordinate, so $r \( y \) = 0$.
    If $r \( y \) = 1$, then $e_0$ belongs to the former range,
    and its image $f \( y \) e_0$ shows that $r \( sigma y \) = 1$.
  Thus $sigma^(- 1) { r = 0 } subset.eq { r = 0 }$ and ${ r = 1 } subset.eq sigma^(- 1) { r =
    1 }$ modulo null sets. Probability preservation makes both inclusions equalities modulo null sets.
    Ergodicity makes each event null or conull. The trace formula and $0 < tau \( p \) < 1$ exclude both conull cases. ◻
]

=== 3.3 A compact-set transfer lemma <a-compact-set-transfer-lemma>

Projection fields need not be continuous. The following observation allows us to pass an identity to a weak limit
  of endpoint measures, provided that their marginals do not concentrate on small sets.
  A family of finite measures is #emph[uniformly absolutely continuous] with respect to $mu$ if,
  for each $epsilon > 0$, one can choose $delta > 0$ such that every set of $mu$-measure below $delta$ has measure
  below $epsilon$ for every member of the family.

#thm([Lemma 3.3.])[
  #emph[Let $X$ be a compact metric space with Borel probability measure $mu$.
    Let $p \( x \)$ be a Borel field of orthogonal projections on $ell^2 \( bb(Z) \)$,
    meaning that every column $x mapsto p \( x \) e_k$ is Borel, and let $F_k : X arrow.r \( 0 \, oo \)$,
    $k in bb(Z)$, be finite Borel functions. Suppose finite positive Borel measures $beta_l$ on $X^2$ converge
    weakly to $beta$. Assume that both families of marginals of $beta_l$ are uniformly absolutely continuous with
    respect to $mu$, and that both marginals of $beta$ are absolutely continuous.
    Suppose also that some $0 < B < oo$ satisfies
$ F_k \( y \) \/ F_k \( x \) lt.eq B quad \( k in bb(Z) \) $

 almost everywhere for each $beta_l$ and for $beta$. If
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.3)")
 $ \( 1 - p \( y \) \) "diag"_k #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] F_k \( y \) \/ F_k \( x \)
   #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] p \( x \) = 0 $ <eq:compact-transfer-identity>]

 holds $beta_l$-almost everywhere for every $l$, then it holds $beta$-almost everywhere.]
] <lem:compact-transfer>

#proof[
  First replace every diagonal entry by its minimum with $B$, so that the diagonal field is bounded everywhere.
    Given an arbitrarily small $epsilon > 0$, simultaneous Lusin approximation supplies a compact $Q subset
    X$ on which every $F_k$ and every vector map $x mapsto p \( x \) e_k$ is continuous,
    while $mu \( X \\ Q \)$ is sufficiently small to make the $beta_l$-mass outside $Q^2$ less than $epsilon$ for
    all $l$. For completeness, the simultaneous assertion follows by applying Lusin’s property to the countably
    many maps with summable exceptional measures and intersecting the resulting compact sets.
    The usual property for maps into separable metric spaces follows by finite-valued approximation and measure
    regularity.
  On $Q^2$, the capped diagonal field is strongly continuous:
    continuity on the basis vectors and its uniform bound imply continuity on every vector.
    The same argument applies to $p \( x \)$ and $p \( y \)$.
    Products of strongly continuous, uniformly bounded fields are strongly continuous.
    Therefore the zero set $Z_Q$ of the capped version of (3.3) in $Q^2$ is compact.
    The assumed identity implies $beta_l \( Z_Q \) gt.eq beta_l \( X^2 \) - epsilon$.
    The compact-set upper bound under weak convergence and convergence of total masses give
  $ beta \( Z_Q \) gt.eq limsup_l beta_l \( Z_Q \) gt.eq beta \( X^2 \) - epsilon . $
  The capped global zero set thus has full $beta$-measure, since $epsilon$ was arbitrary.
    It is measurable by its countably many matrix entries. Finally the cap changes no diagonal entry $beta$-almost
    everywhere. ◻
]

=== 3.4 The measurable weight and its cocycles <the-measurable-weight-and-its-cocycles>

We return to the prescribed rotation on $X = bb(R) \/ bb(Z)$.
  The next formulas supply its positive weight and the diagonal ratios to which Lemma 11 will apply.
  For the parameters of Proposition 8, put

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.4)")
 $ g_(*) \( x \) = sum_j C_j sum_(i in J_j) floor.l u_i \( x \) + alpha_i floor.r \, wide f_(*) \( x \) =
   e^(- g_(*) \( x \)) \, wide T_(*) = U pi \( f_(*) \) . $ <eq:measurable-weight>]

The sum defining $g_(*)$ has only finitely many nonzero terms almost everywhere.
  Here $c_i = C_j$ for $i in J_j$ in the introduction’s notation. To prove the finiteness assertion, observe that

$ sum_i mu { floor.l u_i + alpha_i floor.r eq.not 0 } = sum_i alpha_i lt.eq 2 sum_j K_j^(- 1) < oo . $

The elementary first Borel–Cantelli implication follows here simply by bounding the measure of a union of tail
  events by the sum of their measures. Thus $0 < f_(*) lt.eq 1$ almost everywhere.

For each $k in bb(Z)$, define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.5)")
 $ F_k \( x \) = exp (- sum_j C_j sum_(i in J_j) floor.l u_i \( x \) + k alpha_i floor.r) . $ <eq:cocycle-definition>]

These sums, too, have only finitely many nonzero terms almost everywhere:
  in the tail where $\| k \| alpha_i < 1$, the probability of a nonzero floor is $\| k \| alpha_i$.
  After taking a countable intersection, there is an invariant conull set on which all $F_k$ are finite and
  strictly positive and

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.6)")
 $ F_(v + n) \( x \) = F_v \( x \) F_n \( sigma^v x \) \, wide F_0 = 1 \, wide F_1 = f_(*) . $ <eq:cocycle-identities>]

Indeed $u_i \( sigma^v x \) = { u_i \( x \) + v alpha_i }$,
  so the addition formula for floors proves the identity term by term.
  Since $alpha_i > 0$, $F_k \( x \)$ is nonincreasing in $k$.
  Give these functions arbitrary positive finite Borel values on the remaining null set.

For endpoints in the common conull set, write

$ R_(x \, y) \( k \) = F_k \( y \) \/ F_k \( x \) . $

If $u_i \( x \) lt.eq u_i \( y \)$, the $i$th coordinate contributes at most $1$ to this ratio.
  For $i in J_s$, it contributes $e^(- C_s)$ precisely when

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.7)")
 $ u_i \( x \) < 1 - { k alpha_i } lt.eq u_i \( y \) . $ <eq:ratio-crossing>]

When $k alpha_i$ is integral the displayed event is empty, as it should be.
  Without an order assumption, an individual coordinate contributes at most $e^(C_s)$,
  since the two floors differ by at most one.

=== 3.5 From invariant projections to endpoint identities <from-invariant-projections-to-endpoint-identities>

Suppose $p in M$ is a projection satisfying $\( 1 - p \) T_(*) p = 0$.
  Its range is then invariant under every positive power of $T_(*)$. The formula

$ T_(*) \( x \)^n = S^n "diag"_v F_n \( sigma^v x \) $

and covariance (3.1) show that, for $y = sigma^n x$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.8)")
 $ \( 1 - p \( y \) \) "diag"_v R_(x \, y) \( v \) p \( x \) = 0 wide \( n gt.eq 1 \) . $ <eq:positive-orbit-identity>]

To verify the division involved, the cocycle identity gives $F_n \( sigma^v x \) \/ F_n \( x \) =
  F_v \( sigma^n x \) \/ F_v \( x \)$; the positive divisor $F_n \( x \)$ is finite.
  The resulting diagonal is bounded on each such fiber by $1 \/ F_n \( x \)$, since $F_n lt.eq 1$.
  This bound need not be uniform in $x$.

If $0 < tau \( p \) < 1$, then

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.9)")
 $ 0 < r \( x \) := chevron.l p \( x \) e_0 \, e_0 chevron.r < 1 quad
   upright("for almost every ") x . $ <eq:diagonal-interior>]

This is Lemma 10, applied with $f = f_(*)$, the faithful trace (3.2), and covariance (3.1).

The next lemma is the payoff from the sampling construction.
  It extends (3.8) beyond actual orbit pairs, while retaining a bound on every diagonal entry at once.

#thm([Lemma 3.4.])[
  #emph[Let $p in M$ be a projection satisfying $\( 1 - p \) T_(*) p = 0$.
    For every integer $A gt.eq 1$ and every $t in X$ that is an integer multiple of $1 \/ q_A$, the identity
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.10)")
 $ \( 1 - p \( x + t \) \) "diag"_v R_(x \, x + t) \( v \) p \( x \) = 0 $ <eq:rational-graph-identity>]

 holds for almost every $x$. The exceptional sets may be chosen simultaneously for this countable family of
   translations.]
] <lem:rational-graphs>

#proof[
  Fix $A \, t$. In every sufficiently late stage $j$, put $a = d_(j - 1) gt.eq A$.
    Since $q_A$ divides $q_a$, the sampling set $cal(N)_(j \, t)$ occurs in Proposition 8.
    Write $w = w_(m_j)$ and $w^(\( 2 \)) = w_(m_j)^(\( 2 \))$ for this stage’s kernels in (2.1).
    Average over $n in cal(N)_(j \, t)$ the graph measures $x mapsto \( x \, sigma^n x \)$ with starting density
  $ product_(i in J_j) w #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] u_i \( x \) \, { n alpha_i } #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  By (2.11), both marginal densities tend to $1$ in $L^2 \( mu \)$.
    For the second marginal this uses the second orientation $w^(\( 2 \)) \( z \, v \) = w \( z - v \, v \)$.
    As every translation $n theta$ lies within $ell_j$ of $t$ and $ell_j arrow.r 0$,
    testing continuous functions on $X^2$ shows that these measures converge weakly to the Haar-first graph
    measure of $x mapsto x + t$. At every point of positive density,
  $ 0 < { n alpha_i } < m_j^(- 3 \/ 5) Delta \( u_i \( x \) \) quad \( i in J_j \) \, $
  so there is no wrap and all current-block coordinates are ordered.
  For every selected $n$, remove the same set of starting points, namely those satisfying at least one of
  $ u_i \( x \) gt.eq 1 - q_a ell_j & \( A lt.eq i lt.eq a \) \,\
u_i \( x \) gt.eq 1 - 2 Q_j \/ N_s & \( i in J_s \, med s > j \) . $
  The removed Haar measure is at most
  $ a q_a ell_j + 2 Q_j sum_(s > j) K_s^(- 1) lt.eq \( j + 1 \)^(- 2) + 4 Q_j \/ K_(j + 1) arrow.r 0 . $
  The $L^2$ bound on the averaged first marginal and Cauchy–Schwarz show that the removed weighted mass tends to
    zero. Thus the trimmed measures have the same weak limit.
    Each trimmed marginal is dominated by its untrimmed counterpart,
    so both retain uniform $L^2$ bounds and hence uniform absolute continuity.
  For $A lt.eq i lt.eq a$, the number $q_i t$ is integral. The coordinate advance is therefore $q_i { n theta -
    t } < q_a ell_j$, and trimming prevents a wrap. For future coordinates it is $n alpha_i < 2 Q_j \/ N_s$,
    again without a wrap. Together with the current block, this orders every coordinate $i gt.eq A$.
    Hence, on all trimmed measures,
  $ R_(x \, y) \( v \) lt.eq exp (sum_(i < A) C_(s \( i \))) quad \( v in bb(Z) \) \, wide i in J_(s \( i \)) . $
  The bound is independent of $j$ and $v$. It also holds on the limiting graph, because $u_i \( x + t \) =
    u_i \( x \)$ for $i gt.eq A$. The positive-orbit identity holds on the approximants, so Lemma 11 gives (3.10).
    There are only countably many pairs $\( A \, t \)$ under consideration. ◻
]

== 4 Ordered pairs from independent digits <sec:couplings>

The rational-translation identities of Lemma 12 will now be transferred to pairs with many opportunities to cross
  the thresholds in (3.7). If both endpoint laws were Haar,
  coordinate order would make each nonnegative difference $u_i \( y \) - u_i \( x \)$ have mean zero,
  leaving no threshold crossings. We therefore allow a controlled change in the endpoint marginals.
  What matters is a uniform square-integrable bound for their marginal densities and a product structure that
  survives conditioning on either endpoint. We first isolate the density estimate,
  which also applies to the independent-coordinate construction in the appendix.

#thm([Lemma 4.1.])[
  (Densities of independent coordinates).  #emph[Let $\( Omega_i \, cal(F)_i \, mu_i \)$, $i gt.eq 1$,
    be probability spaces and let $h_i gt.eq 0$ satisfy $integral h_i thin d mu_i = 1$. If
$ sum_(i gt.eq 1) parallel h_i - 1 parallel_(L^2 \( mu_i \))^2 lt.eq C < oo \, $

 then the product probability measure $nu = ⨂_(i gt.eq 1) \( h_i mu_i \)$ has a density with respect to
   $mu = ⨂_(i gt.eq 1) mu_i$ whose $L^2 \( mu \)$ norm is at most $e^(C \/ 2)$.]
  #emph[In particular, suppose two probability densities $h_i^0 \, h_i^1$ at each coordinate satisfy
$ parallel h_i^a - 1 parallel_(L^2 \( mu_i \))^2 lt.eq b_i quad \( a = 0 \, 1 \) \, wide sum_(i gt.eq 1) b_i lt.eq C . $

 For every deterministic sequence $epsilon.alt_i in { 0 \, 1 }$,
   the product law with coordinate densities $h_i^(epsilon.alt_i)$ has the same bound.]
] <lem:product-density-bound>

#proof[
  The mean-one assumption gives $parallel h_i parallel_2^2 = 1 + parallel h_i - 1 parallel_2^2$.
    Hence each finite product density obeys
  $ ∥product_(i = 1)^n h_i∥_(L^2 \( mu \))^2 = product_(i = 1)^n #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 1 + parallel h_i -
    1 parallel_2^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] lt.eq exp (sum_(i = 1)^n parallel h_i - 1 parallel_2^2) lt.eq e^C . $
  For a bounded cylinder function $phi$ depending on the first $n$ coordinates,
    integration under $nu$ is therefore bounded in absolute value by $e^(C \/ 2) parallel phi
    parallel_(L^2 \( mu \))$. These cylinder functions are dense in $L^2 \( mu \)$,
    so the integration functional extends continuously to that space.
    The Riesz representation theorem gives a real $H in L^2 \( mu \)$ with norm at most $e^(C \/ 2)$ representing
    the extension. The finite signed measure $H mu$ and the probability measure $nu$ agree on all cylinder sets.
    Uniqueness of finite measures on the product sigma-algebra gives $H mu = nu$; thus $H gt.eq
    0$ almost everywhere and $integral H thin d mu = 1$. Applying the first assertion to $h_i =
    h_i^(epsilon.alt_i)$ proves the hybrid assertion. ◻
]

Write $r_i^(*) = q_(i + 1) \/ q_i$. A Haar-uniform point of $X$ has the expansion

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.1)")
 $ x = A_0 / q_1 + sum_(i gt.eq 1) A_i / q_(i + 1) \, wide A_0 in { 0 \, dots.h \, q_1 - 1 } \, quad A_i in { 0 \,
   dots.h \, r_i^(*) - 1 } \, $ <eq:digit-expansion>]

with independent uniform digits. Indeed, specifying a finite prefix selects an interval whose length is the
  reciprocal of the product of the relevant radices. Conversely these intervals determine the Haar measure.
  The ambiguity at endpoints of such intervals is a null set.
  In particular, $A_i = floor.l r_i^(*) u_i \( x \) floor.r$,
  and $u_i \( x \)$ is the tail of (4.1) starting with digit $A_i$, after multiplication by $q_i$.
  We couple the independent digits; each character coordinate $u_i$ depends on an overlapping tail of these digits.

Fix a block index $j$. We construct a probability law $bb(P)_j$ on a pair $\( x \, y \)$,
  retaining auxiliary labels. Use the same uniform initial digit for both endpoints.
  Most coordinate pairs will have equal raw values. Rare top and bottom components force crossings even after one
  endpoint has been fixed, and an extra component in block $j$ places the endpoints close to opposite ends of the
  unit interval. These components give tail attenuation while keeping the marginal densities uniformly square
  integrable. Independently at every $i in J_s$, choose a raw pair $\( X_i \, Y_i \)$ by the following mixture, where

$ rho_s \( w \) = kappa_s sqrt(w / m_s) . $

For each of the two labelled widths $w = a_s \, b_s$, include these two components, each of probability $rho_s \( w \)$:

$ upright("top:") quad & X_i upright(" uniform on ") \[ 0 \, 1 - w \/ 2 \) \, & Y_i
  upright(" uniform on ") \[ 1 - w \/ 2 \, 1 \) \,\
upright("bottom:") quad & X_i upright(" uniform on ") \[ 0 \, w \/ 2 \) \, & Y_i
  upright(" uniform on ") \[ w \/ 2 \, 1 \) . $

The two choices within each displayed component are independent.
  Keep the width labels distinct even if their numerical values coincide.
  If $s = j$, also include a #emph[tagged] component of probability $1 \/ m_s$, with independent uniforms

$ X_i in \[ 0 \, 1 \/ \( 2 m_s \) \) \, wide Y_i in \[ 1 - 1 \/ \( 2 m_s \) \, 1 \) . $

With the remaining probability set $X_i = Y_i$ to a uniform point of $\[ 0 \, 1 \)$.
  These probabilities have a positive remainder: the parameter bounds give $rho_s \( w \) \/ w lt.eq kappa_s$,
  $w lt.eq 1 \/ 4$, and $m_s gt.eq 200$. Define the endpoint digits by

$ A_i = floor.l r_i^(*) X_i floor.r \, wide B_i = floor.l r_i^(*) Y_i floor.r \, $

and use (4.1) to form $x$ from the $A_i$ and $y$ from the $B_i$.
  Thus $X_i \, Y_i$ choose the first digit of the corresponding circle coordinates;
  they are not themselves those coordinates.

#thm([Lemma 4.2.])[
  (Uniform marginal bounds).  #emph[There is a finite constant $H_0$, independent of $j$,
    such that the marginal laws of $x$ and $y$ have densities with respect to $mu$ of $L^2 \( mu \)$ norm at most
    $H_0$. The same assertion holds for an endpoint formed by any deterministic choice of the $x$-digit or the
    $y$-digit at each coordinate, retaining the common uniform initial digit.]
] <lem:digit-marginals>

#proof[
  Either raw marginal density is at most $10$. For each width its top and bottom contributions together are at
    most $4 rho_s \( w \) \/ w$, the tag contributes at most $2$, and the diagonal component contributes at most $1$.
  We also need a sharper integral estimate. A uniform density on an interval of length at least $w \/
    2$ differs from $1$ by a mean-zero function of squared $L^2$ norm at most $2 \/ w$.
    For a tagged interval the corresponding bound is $2 m_s$.
    Express a raw marginal density minus $1$ as the sum of its at most five weighted mean-zero perturbations.
    The finite-sum Cauchy–Schwarz inequality gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.2)")
   $ integral_0^1 d_i \( t \)^2 thin d t lt.eq 1 + 50 (kappa_s^2 / m_s + upright(bold(1))_({ s = j }) / m_s) \,
     wide i in J_s . $ <eq:one-digit-square>]
  In fact the four width terms contribute at most $40 kappa_s^2 \/ m_s$, and the tag at most $10 \/ m_s$.
    Passing from raw variables to digits replaces $d_i$ by its average on each radix bin,
    so Jensen’s inequality preserves (4.2).
  Use the uniform law at each digit as the reference probability measure.
    The pairs of digits are independent across coordinates, and each endpoint digit density has mean one.
    Since $\| J_s \| = m_s$, summing (4.2) minus one over the coordinates gives the common bound
    $50 \( sum_s kappa_s^2 + 1 \)$, for either endpoint and every deterministic choice between them.
    The initial digit has density one. Lemma 13 therefore gives the bound
  $ H_0^2 := exp (50 (sum_(s gt.eq 1) kappa_s^2 + 1)) < oo . $
  Outside the null sets of ambiguous expansions, the mixed-radix map is a measure-preserving bijection from the
    reference digit space to $X$. Transporting the density through this map gives a Haar density with the same
    $L^2$ norm. This proves the assertion for both endpoints and all the deterministic hybrids. ◻
]

All the endpoint expansions consequently avoid their ambiguous values almost surely.
  Every raw pair satisfies $X_i lt.eq Y_i$, so $A_i lt.eq B_i$. The nonnegative tail expansion then gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.3)")
 $ u_i \( x \) lt.eq u_i \( y \) \, wide \| u_i \( x \) - X_i \| lt.eq 1 / r_i^(*) \, wide \| u_i \( y \) -
   Y_i \| lt.eq 1 / r_i^(*) quad \( i gt.eq 1 \) . $ <eq:coordinate-order>]

For $D gt.eq 1$, let $y^(\( D \))$ have $y$’s digits at indices $1 \, dots.h \, D$ and $x$’s later digits.
  Then $u_i \( x \) lt.eq u_i \( y^(\( D \)) \)$ for all $i$, $y^(\( D \)) arrow.r y$, and $y^(\( D \)) -
  x$ is a multiple of $1 \/ q_(D + 1)$.

#thm([Proposition 4.3.])[
  (Invariance on the ordered pairs).  #emph[Let $p in M$ be a projection satisfying $\( 1 - p \) T_(*) p = 0$.
    For every $j$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.4)")
 $ \( 1 - p \( y \) \) "diag"_(k in bb(Z)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] R_(x \, y) \( k \)
   #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] p \( x \) = 0 quad upright("for ") bb(P)_j upright("-almost every pair") .
   $ <eq:coupling-identity>]

 Moreover $0 < R_(x \, y) \( k \) lt.eq 1$ for all $k in bb(Z)$ almost surely.]
] <prop:ordered-couplings>

#proof[
  Fix $D$. The rational shifts $y^(\( D \)) - x$ have only finitely many possible values.
    Lemma 12 holds outside a common Haar-null set for all these shifts,
    so the absolute continuity of the $x$ marginal implies the required identity at $\( x \, y^(\( D \)) \)$.
    This remains valid even though the selected shift depends on the pair.
  The coordinate order and (3.7) bound every ratio by $1$, both for these pairs and for their limit
    $\( x \, y \)$. Their measures converge weakly by the almost-sure convergence $y^(\( D \)) arrow.r y$.
    Lemma 14 supplies uniform $L^2$ bounds for both marginals.
    In particular their masses on a set of Haar measure $delta$ are at most $H_0 sqrt(delta)$.
    Lemma 11 now passes the identity to the limit. ◻
]

We have transferred the invariance identity to ordered pairs.
  The remaining reason for choosing these particular laws is that an interior threshold has a quantitatively
  positive chance of being crossed, even after one entire endpoint has been fixed.

#thm([Lemma 4.4.])[
  (Conditional labels and forced crossings).  #emph[Conditional on $x$,
    all coordinate labels are independent and each posterior depends only on the corresponding digit $A_i$.
    The analogous statement holds conditional on $y$, using $B_i$.]
  #emph[For $i in J_s$, $w in { a_s \, b_s }$, and $u in \[ w \, 1 - w \]$, the following bounds hold almost surely:
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.5)")
 $ bb(P)_j \( upright("top label of width ") w upright(" at ") i divides x \) & gt.eq
   rho_s \( w \) \/ 10 &  & upright("if ") u_i \( x \) < u \,\
bb(P)_j \( upright("bottom label of width ") w upright(" at ") i divides y \) & gt.eq
  rho_s \( w \) \/ 10 &  & upright("if ") u_i \( y \) gt.eq u . $ <eq:conditional-crossing>]

 In the first case that label forces $u_i \( y \) gt.eq u$; in the second it forces $u_i \( x \) < u$.]
] <lem:conditional-labels>

#proof[
  The entire endpoint determines all its digits almost surely.
    For any finite collection of labels, conditioning on all $x$-digits gives the product of the individual posteriors
  $
    frac(bb(P)_j \( upright("label ") ell upright(" at ") i \) thin bb(P)_j \( A_i = a divides upright("label ")
    ell upright(" at ") i \), bb(P)_j \( A_i = a \)) . $
  One verifies this formula first against cylinder tests in the digit sequence,
    using independence of coordinate pairs, and then against all bounded digit-measurable tests by the monotone
    class theorem. The common initial digit is independent of those pairs.
    This proves conditional independence given $x$; the proof for $y$ is the same.
    In particular, the dependence of $u_i \( x \)$ on later digits does not change the label posteriors after the
    entire endpoint has been conditioned on.
  The parameters satisfy
  $ 1 / r_i^(*) lt.eq 1 / N_s < w / 2 . $
  If $u_i \( x \) < u lt.eq 1 - w$, the whole radix bin containing $X_i$ lies in $\[ 0 \, 1 - w \/ 2 \)$.
    On that bin the top component’s $X_i$-density is at least $1$,
    while the total raw marginal density is at most $10$. Its posterior probability is therefore at least
    $rho_s \( w \) \/ 10$. Under this label, (4.3) gives
  $ u_i \( y \) gt.eq 1 - w \/ 2 - 1 \/ r_i^(*) > 1 - w gt.eq u . $
  For the second assertion, $u_i \( y \) gt.eq u gt.eq w$ places the entire $Y_i$ bin in $\[ w \/ 2 \, 1 \)$.
    The bottom component again has density at least $1$ there, so its posterior is at least $rho_s \( w \) \/ 10$.
    Its $X_i$ value satisfies
  $ u_i \( x \) lt.eq w \/ 2 + 1 \/ r_i^(*) < w lt.eq u . $
  These estimates include thresholds at radix-bin endpoints. ◻
]

== 5 The convolution obstruction <sec:obstruction>

We can now exclude every nontrivial invariant projection for the measurable weight.
  The argument compares two estimates for a finite part of a convolution:
  polynomial compactness prevents that part from vanishing,
  whereas the ordered couplings make its complementary tail too small to cancel it.

=== 5.1 A conditional convolution estimate <a-conditional-convolution-estimate>

We isolate the common argument so that the product construction can use it without repeating the infinite-tail
  estimate. The probability space in the lemma may include auxiliary labels besides its endpoints.

#thm([Lemma 5.1.])[
  (Conditional convolution obstruction).  #emph[Let $K gt.eq 1$ be an integer, $D gt.eq a > 0$, $c > 0$,
    and $eta \, delta gt.eq 0$. On a probability space with endpoints $x \, y$, let $xi \( x \) \,
    gamma \( y \)$ be measurable vectors in the unit ball of $ell^2 \( bb(Z) \)$,
    and let $F_k$ be positive finite measurable functions of an endpoint, with $F_0 = 1$. Set
$ A_v = frac(xi_v \( x \), F_v \( x \)) \, wide B_w = F_(- w) \( y \) overline(gamma_(- w) \( y \)) \, wide
  R \( k \) = frac(F_k \( y \), F_k \( x \)) . $

 Suppose, almost surely, for $0 lt.eq n lt.eq 2 K$ and every $v in bb(Z)$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.1)")
 $ sum_(v in bb(Z)) A_v B_(- n - v) = 0 \, wide 0 < frac(F_(n + v) \( y \), F_v \( x \)) lt.eq min { R \( v \) \,
   R \( n + v \) } lt.eq 1 . $ <eq:generic-convolution>]

 Let $E$ be an event of probability at least $c$ on which
$ \| A_v \| \, \| B_v \| lt.eq D quad \( \| v \| lt.eq K \) \, wide \| A_0 \| \, \| B_0 \| gt.eq a . $

 For each $\| k \| > K$, suppose there are events $H_k^(\( 1 \)) \, H_k^(\( 2 \))$ with
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.2)")
 $ R \( k \) lt.eq eta + upright(bold(1))_(H_k^(\( 1 \))) + upright(bold(1))_(H_k^(\( 2 \))) quad
   upright("on ") E \, wide bb(P) \( H_k^(\( 1 \)) divides x \) lt.eq delta \, quad
   bb(P) \( H_k^(\( 2 \)) divides y \) lt.eq delta . $ <eq:generic-tail>]

 The conditional bounds are under the full probability law, before restriction to $E$.
   If $epsilon \( K \, D \, a \)$ is the positive constant from Lemma 7, then
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.3)")
 $ c epsilon \( K \, D \, a \) lt.eq \( 2 K + 1 \) \( eta + 2 sqrt(delta) \) . $ <eq:generic-obstruction>]

]
] <lem:conditional-convolution>

#proof[
  The multiplier bound in (5.1) and Cauchy–Schwarz make every convolution there absolutely convergent. Put
  $ c_n = sum_(\| v \| lt.eq K\
\| n + v \| lt.eq K) A_v B_(- n - v) quad \( 0 lt.eq n lt.eq 2 K \) . $
  On $E$, Lemma 7 applied to the two truncated Laurent polynomials gives $sum_(n = 0)^(2 K) \| c_n \| gt.eq
    epsilon \( K \, D \, a \)$. Its expected value on $E$ is therefore at least $c epsilon \( K \, D \, a \)$.
  Fix $n$. For an omitted index $v$, choose $k \( v \) = v$ if $\| v \| > K$, and $k \( v \) = n + v$ otherwise.
    This is a deterministic choice with $\| k \( v \) \| > K$. The zero convolution and the two multiplier bounds give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.4)")
   $ upright(bold(1))_E \| c_n \| lt.eq eta + sum_(b = 1)^2 sum_(v upright(" omitted")) \| xi_v \( x \)
     gamma_(n + v) \( y \) \| upright(bold(1))_(H_(k \( v \))^(\( b \))) . $ <eq:masked-tail>]
  The unmasked sum of products of vector coordinates is at most one, which accounts for $eta$.
    We dropped $upright(bold(1))_E$ from the nonnegative exceptional terms so that their full-law conditional
    bounds remain applicable.
  For the first exceptional sum, Cauchy–Schwarz on probability times counting measure bounds its expectation by
  $ (bb(E) sum_(v upright(" omitted")) \| xi_v \( x \) \|^2 upright(bold(1))_(H_(k \( v \))^(\( 1 \))))^(1 \/ 2)
    (bb(E) sum_(v upright(" omitted")) \| gamma_(n + v) \( y \) \|^2)^(1 \/ 2) lt.eq sqrt(delta) . $
  Indeed, condition the first factor’s square on $x$, apply (5.2) to each deterministic $k \( v \)$,
    and use $sum_v \| xi_v \( x \) \|^2 lt.eq 1$. The second factor is at most one.
    For the other exceptional sum put its indicator with $\| gamma_(n + v) \( y \) \|^2$ and condition on $y$,
    giving the same bound. Tonelli’s theorem justifies every interchange of the nonnegative sums and integrals.
    No sum of the individual exceptional probabilities is required.
    Taking expectations in (5.4) and summing in $n$ proves (5.3). ◻
]

=== 5.2 Application to the circle weight <application-to-the-circle-weight>

#thm([Theorem 5.2.])[
  #emph[The bounded operator $T_(*) = U pi \( f_(*) \)$ has no projection $p in M$ satisfying $0 < tau \( p \) <
    1$ and $\( 1 - p \) T_(*) p = 0$.]
] <thm:measurable>

Suppose, for contradiction, that such a projection exists. Put

$ xi \( x \) = p \( x \) e_0 \, wide gamma \( y \) = \( 1 - p \( y \) \) e_0 \, wide r \( x \) = p \( x \)_(0 \, 0) . $

Both vector norms are at most $1$. We shall use the laws $bb(P)_j$ of Section 4;
  their expectations are denoted by $bb(E)_j$.

=== 5.3 The exact convolution identities <the-exact-convolution-identities>

By (4.4), the vector $"diag"_v \( R_(x \, y) \( v \) \) xi \( x \)$ belongs to $"ran" p \( y \)$.
  Applying $T_(*) \( y \)^n$, which preserves that range for $n gt.eq 0$, and pairing with $gamma \( y \)$ gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.5)")
 $ 0 = sum_(v in bb(Z)) frac(F_(n + v) \( y \), F_v \( x \)) xi_v \( x \) overline(gamma_(n + v) \( y \)) =
   sum_(v in bb(Z)) A_v B_(- n - v) \, wide n gt.eq 0 \, $ <eq:full-convolution>]

where, in this section,

$ A_v = frac(xi_v \( x \), F_v \( x \)) \, wide B_v = F_(- v) \( y \) overline(gamma_(- v) \( y \)) . $

All these identities hold simultaneously almost surely under each $bb(P)_j$. They are absolutely convergent.
  Indeed, monotonicity of $F_k$ in $k$ and coordinate order imply

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.6)")
 $ frac(F_(n + v) \( y \), F_v \( x \)) lt.eq min { R_(x \, y) \( v \) \, R_(x \, y) \( n + v \) } lt.eq 1 \,
   $ <eq:ratio-minimum>]

and $sum_v \| xi_v \( x \) gamma_(n + v) \( y \) \| lt.eq 1$ by Cauchy–Schwarz.
  The comparison with $R_(x \, y) \( v \)$ follows from $F_(n + v) \( y \) lt.eq F_v \( y \)$;
  the comparison with $R_(x \, y) \( n + v \)$ follows from $F_(n + v) \( x \) lt.eq F_v \( x \)$.

=== 5.4 A finite convolution cannot be too small <a-finite-convolution-cannot-be-too-small>

Fix $j$ and abbreviate $K = K_j$, $m = m_j$. Define an event $E_j$ by the following three conditions:

$ & X_i < 1 - 3 \/ m \, quad Y_i > 3 \/ m quad \( i in J_j \) \, quad upright("and at least one tag in ") J_j \;\
 & X_i < 1 - 3 K \/ N_s \, quad Y_i > 3 K \/ N_s quad \( i in J_s \, med s > j \) \;\
 & r \( x \) gt.eq 1 \/ j \, wide 1 - r \( y \) gt.eq 1 \/ j . $

For $0 lt.eq n lt.eq 2 K$, let

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.7)")
 $ c_(j \, n) = sum_(\| v \| lt.eq K\
\| n + v \| lt.eq K) A_v B_(- n - v) . $ <eq:truncated-convolution>]

#thm([Lemma 5.3.])[
  #emph[There is a constant $c_(*) > 0$ such that, for all sufficiently large $j$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.8)")
 $ bb(P)_j \( E_j \) gt.eq c_(*) \, wide bb(E)_j [upright(bold(1))_(E_j) sum_(n = 0)^(2 K_j) \| c_(j \, n) \|]
   gt.eq c_(*) epsilon_j . $ <eq:finite-lower-bound>]

 The point after which these inequalities hold may depend on $p$.]
] <lem:truncation-bound>

#proof[
  At a current-block coordinate, a tag has probability $1 \/ m$ and satisfies both endpoint exclusions.
    Since each raw marginal density is at most $10$, the probability of an inadmissible outcome is at most $60 \/
    m$. Thus a nontagged admissible outcome has probability at least $1 - 61 \/ m$.
    Independence across the block shows that exactly one tag and admissible nontagged outcomes at the other
    coordinates have probability at least
  $ m 1 / m \( 1 - 61 \/ m \)^(m - 1) gt.eq e^(- 90) wide \( m gt.eq 200 \) . $
  For example, the last estimate follows from $log \( 1 - t \) gt.eq - t \/ \( 1 - t \)$ with $t = 61 \/ m$.
  The total probability excluded by the future-block inequalities is at most
  $ 60 K_j sum_(s > j) 1 / K_s lt.eq frac(120 K_j, K_(j + 1)) arrow.r 0 . $
  By (3.9), $0 < r < 1$ Haar-almost everywhere. Lemma 14 therefore bounds the probability lost from the last line
    of the definition of $E_j$ by
  $ H_0 mu { r < 1 \/ j }^(1 \/ 2) + H_0 mu { 1 - r < 1 \/ j }^(1 \/ 2) arrow.r 0 . $
  Consequently $c_(*) = e^(- 90) \/ 2$ works for all sufficiently large $j$.
  On $E_j$, rounding changes the raw endpoint inequalities by at most $1 \/ N_s$. Since $K gt.eq 1$, they imply
  $ u_i \( x \) < 1 - 2 K \/ N_s \, wide u_i \( y \) > 2 K \/ N_s quad \( i in J_s \, med s gt.eq j \) . $
  For $0 lt.eq v lt.eq K$, the bound $alpha_i < 2 \/ N_s$ makes the floors of $u_i \( x \) +
    v alpha_i$ and $u_i \( y \) - v alpha_i$ zero in every such block.
    Earlier-block floors have absolute value at most $K + 1$. Hence
  $ 1 \/ F_v \( x \) lt.eq D_j \, wide F_(- v) \( y \) lt.eq D_j quad \( 0 lt.eq v lt.eq K \) . $
  For $v < 0$ these two quantities are at most $1$, by monotonicity and $F_0 = 1$.
    Since the vector coordinates have modulus at most $1$, all coefficients of the Laurent polynomials
  $ A^(\[ K \]) \( z \) = sum_(\| v \| lt.eq K) A_v z^v \, wide B^(\[ K \]) \( z \) = sum_(\| v \| lt.eq K) B_v z^v $
  have modulus at most $D_j$. Their constant coefficients are respectively $r \( x \)$ and $1 - r \( y \)$,
    both at least $1 \/ j$ on $E_j$. The coefficient of $z^(- n)$ in their product is $c_(j \, n)$.
    The polynomial bound (2.10) now gives $sum_(n = 0)^(2 K) \| c_(j \, n) \| gt.eq epsilon_j$ on $E_j$.
    Integration proves the second assertion. ◻
]

=== 5.5 Controlling every part of the infinite tail <controlling-every-part-of-the-infinite-tail>

To contradict (5.8), we must bound the omitted terms of (5.5).
  The important estimate is conditional on one endpoint. It will let us sum infinitely many terms without taking a
  union bound over their exceptional events.

#thm([Lemma 5.4.])[
  #emph[For every $j$ and every integer $k$ with $\| k \| > K_j$, there are measurable events $H_k^(\( 1 \)) \,
    H_k^(\( 2 \))$ in the labelled coupling space such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.9)")
 $ bb(P)_j \( H_k^(\( 1 \)) divides x \) lt.eq e^(- L_j \/ 400) \, wide
   bb(P)_j \( H_k^(\( 2 \)) divides y \) lt.eq e^(- L_j \/ 400) quad upright("almost surely") \,
   $ <eq:conditional-bad-events>]

 and on $E_j$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.10)")
 $ R_(x \, y) \( k \) lt.eq e^(- L_j) + upright(bold(1))_(H_k^(\( 1 \))) +
   upright(bold(1))_(H_k^(\( 2 \))) . $ <eq:tail-ratio-bound>]

 The conditional bounds hold before any restriction to $E_j$.]
] <lem:tail-attenuation>

#proof[
  First suppose $K_j < \| k \| lt.eq N_j \/ 4$. A tagged coordinate in $J_j$ satisfies, after rounding,
  $ u_i \( x \) < 1 \/ m_j \, wide u_i \( y \) > 1 - 1 \/ m_j . $
  Here $beta = \| k \| alpha_i$ lies in $\( 1 \/ m_j \, 1 \/ 2 \)$.
    The threshold $1 - { k alpha_i }$ equals $1 - beta$ for $k > 0$ and $beta$ for $k < 0$.
    It is crossed in either case, so (3.7) gives $R_(x \, y) \( k \) lt.eq e^(- C_j) lt.eq e^(- L_j)$ on $E_j$.
    Set both exceptional events to be empty in this range.
  We next cover all larger $\| k \|$. Put $M_s = m_s^(\( 4 \/ 5 \) m_s)$. The intervals
  $ \[ N_j \/ 4 \, M_j \] \, wide \[ G_s K_s \, M_s \] quad \( s > j \) $
  cover $\[ N_j \/ 4 \, oo \)$. Indeed the next left endpoint is below the preceding right endpoint by (2.9),
    the right endpoints tend to infinity, and $G_s K_s lt.eq N_s \/ 4$ because $m_s gt.eq 4 G_s$.
    Split each later interval at $N_s \/ 4$. In its lower part the thresholds belong to $\[ a_s \, 1 - a_s \]$ at
    every coordinate of $J_s$. In its upper part, (2.8) places them in $\[ b_s \, 1 - b_s \]$ at at least $m_s \/
    20$ coordinates. The initial interval uses the upper-part assertion for $s = j$.
  Thus for each fixed $k$ in this tail we can choose, deterministically, a block $J_s$, a width $w in { a_s \,
    b_s }$, and a set $I subset J_s$ of at least $m_s \/ 20$ indices such that
  $ u_i^(*) := 1 - { k alpha_i } in \[ w \, 1 - w \] quad \( i in I \) . $
  The lower-part choice always has $s > j$. In either type of choice,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.11)")
   $ m_s rho_s \( w \) gt.eq L_j \, wide C_s gt.eq L_j . $ <eq:crossing-intensity>]
  For $w = b_s$ the first quantity is $kappa_s m_s^(1 \/ 40) gt.eq L_s gt.eq L_j$; for $w =
    a_s$ it is $kappa_s sqrt(G_s) gt.eq L_(s - 1) gt.eq L_j$.
  Let $H_k$ be the event of no crossing at any index in $I$, and define
  $ H_k^(\( 1 \)) = H_k ∩ {\# { i in I : u_i \( x \) < u_i^(*) } gt.eq \| I \| \/ 2} \, wide H_k^(\( 2 \)) =
    H_k \\ H_k^(\( 1 \)) . $
  Conditional on $x$, the first event forbids the top label at every index in the indicated $x$-measurable set.
    Conditional independence and Lemma 16 therefore give
  $ bb(P)_j \( H_k^(\( 1 \)) divides x \) lt.eq \( 1 - rho_s \( w \) \/ 10 \)^(\| I \| \/ 2) lt.eq
    e^(- m_s rho_s \( w \) \/ 400) lt.eq e^(- L_j \/ 400) . $
  If the $x$-measurable set has fewer than $\| I \| \/ 2$ elements, the event is empty, so the same bound holds.
  For the other conditional estimate, note explicitly that coordinate order and the absence of crossings imply
  $ u_i \( x \) < u_i^(*) quad arrow.l.r.double quad u_i \( y \) < u_i^(*) wide \( i in I \) . $
  Thus $H_k^(\( 2 \))$ requires at least $\| I \| \/ 2$ indices with $u_i \( y \) gt.eq u_i^(*)$.
    Conditional on $y$, the bottom labels at all those indices are forbidden.
    The same product estimate proves the second bound in (5.9).
    These arguments use the original law, without conditioning on $E_j$. Figure 1 displays the complementary split.
  #figure(
    [#cetz.canvas({
  import cetz.draw: *
  content((0, 0), [#box(width: 8.623cm, inset: 0.2117cm, radius: 4pt, stroke: (thickness: 0.4pt, miter-limit: 10), [#set text(hyphenate: false, top-edge: "bounds", bottom-edge: "bounds"); #align(center)[$H_k$: no listed threshold is crossed#linebreak()On this event, $u_i(x)<u_i^* arrow.l.r.double u_i(y)<u_i^*$.]])])
