=== 5.6 Completion of the contradiction <completion-of-the-contradiction>

Apply Lemma 17 to the labelled law $bb(P)_j$, with

$ K = K_j \, quad D = D_j \, quad a = 1 \/ j \, quad c = c_(*) = e^(- 90) \/ 2 \, wide eta = e^(- L_j) \, quad
  delta = e^(- L_j \/ 400) . $

Equations (5.5) and (5.6) verify its algebraic assumptions.
  The proof of Lemma 19 supplies the event of mass at least $c_(*)$ and all central-coefficient bounds for
  sufficiently large $j$. Lemma 20 gives the tail estimate with conditional probabilities under the full law.
  Consequently

$ c_(*) epsilon_j lt.eq \( 2 K_j + 1 \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] e^(- L_j) +
  2 e^(- L_j \/ 800) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] lt.eq epsilon_j / j \, $

where the last inequality is the parameter choice (2.10).
  Since $epsilon_j > 0$ and eventually $1 \/ j < c_(*)$, this is impossible. This proves Theorem 18.

== 6 A continuous weight with one zero <sec:continuity>

The measurable obstruction of Theorem 18 is now in place.
  We will replace its logarithmic cost $g_(*)$ by a function continuous off one point and tending to infinity at
  that point. The replacement changes the cost by the constant $1$ plus a measurable coboundary.
  Since its transfer function need not be bounded, we begin with the precise operator statement that will preserve
  the obstruction.

=== 6.1 Transport by an affiliated positive operator <transport-by-an-affiliated-positive-operator>

A positive self-adjoint operator is #emph[affiliated] with a von Neumann algebra if all its spectral projections
  belong to that algebra. Such an operator commutes, including on its domain, with every unitary in the commutant.
  The action of an invertible affiliated operator on projection lattices also appears in
  #cite(label("mori"), form: "prose") Proposition 3.1. The form needed here includes a specified intertwining
  domain and preservation of the trace; we give a direct spectral-cutoff proof.

#thm([Lemma 6.1.])[
  #emph[Let $\( cal(M) \, tau \)$ be a finite von Neumann algebra acting on a Hilbert space,
    with faithful normal tracial state $tau$. Let $H$ be an injective positive self-adjoint operator affiliated
    with $cal(M)$, and let $A \, B in cal(M)$. Suppose
$ A \( "Dom" H \) subset.eq "Dom" H \, wide H A zeta = B H zeta quad \( zeta in "Dom" H \) . $

 For every projection $p in cal(M)$ with $A "ran" p subset.eq "ran" p$, the projection $P$ onto
$ overline(H \( "ran" p ∩ "Dom" H \)) $

 belongs to $cal(M)$, is invariant under $B$, and satisfies $tau \( P \) = tau \( p \)$.]
] <lem:affiliated-transport>

#proof[
  Every commutant unitary preserves $"ran" p$ and $"Dom" H$, and commutes with $H$ on that domain.
    It therefore maps the displayed closed space onto itself.
    Its projection commutes with the commutant, proving $P in cal(M)$.
    If $zeta in "ran" p ∩ "Dom" H$, both invariance assumptions give $A zeta in "ran" p ∩ "Dom" H$,
    and $B H zeta = H A zeta$. Thus $B$ preserves the unclosed image and, by boundedness, its closure.
  It remains to prove the trace assertion; bounded similarity cannot be assumed.
    Put $E_N = upright(bold(1))_(\[ 1 \/ N \, N \]) \( H \)$. Since $H$ is injective, $E_N arrow.t 1$.
    For $q_N = p and E_N$, the bounded element $H q_N = \( H E_N \) q_N$ of $cal(M)$ is bounded below by $1 \/
    N$ on its initial space. Its image is closed, and its polar decomposition identifies the initial projection
    $q_N$ with its final projection, which is dominated by $P$. Therefore
  $ tau \( P \) gt.eq tau \( p and E_N \) gt.eq tau \( p \) + tau \( E_N \) - 1 . $
  The last inequality follows directly from the polar decomposition of $\( 1 - E_N \) p$:
    its initial support is $p - \( p and E_N \)$ and its final support is at most $1 - E_N$.
    Letting $N$ increase gives $tau \( P \) gt.eq tau \( p \)$.
  For the reverse inequality, set $q'_N = \( 1 - p \) and E_N$.
    The bounded operator $H^(- 1) q'_N = \( H^(- 1) E_N \) q'_N$ is likewise bounded below on its initial space
    and has closed image. This image is orthogonal to $"ran" P$.
    Indeed, for $eta in "ran" q'_N$ and $zeta in "ran" p ∩ "Dom" H$, self-adjointness gives
  $ chevron.l H^(- 1) eta \, H zeta chevron.r = chevron.l eta \, zeta chevron.r = 0 \; $
  here $H^(- 1) eta$ lies in $"Dom" H$. Polar decomposition and the same meet estimate yield
  $ tau \( 1 - P \) gt.eq tau \( \( 1 - p \) and E_N \) gt.eq tau \( 1 - p \) + tau \( E_N \) - 1 . $
  Passing to the limit proves the opposite inequality for $tau \( P \)$. ◻
]

=== 6.2 Smoothing and moving the logarithmic drops <smoothing-and-moving-the-logarithmic-drops>

We claim that there exist a nonnegative function $g$, finite and continuous on $X \\ {
  0 }$ and tending to infinity at $0$, and an almost everywhere finite real measurable function $b$ such that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.1)")
 $ g = g_(*) + 1 + b compose sigma - b quad upright("almost everywhere") \, wide integral_X g thin d mu =
   oo . $ <eq:continuous-coboundary>]

The construction has three parts: smooth each drop, move its support into a shrinking arc,
  and add an integrable background cost that guarantees divergence at the center of those arcs.
  Apart from the constant $1$, all changes will be explicit coboundaries.

Fix $i in J_j$ and put $delta_i = alpha_i \/ 4$. For $u in X$,
  define the continuous periodic smoothing of the sawtooth by

$ s_i \( u \) = frac(1, 2 delta_i) integral_(- delta_i)^(delta_i) { u + t } thin d t . $

It equals ${ u }$ outside the $delta_i$-neighborhood of the cut at $0$. Set

$ h_i \( x \) = C_j #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] alpha_i + s_i \( u_i \( x \) \) -
  s_i \( u_i \( x \) + alpha_i \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $

The elementary identity $floor.l { u } + alpha_i floor.r = alpha_i + { u } - { u +
  alpha_i }$ shows that $h_i$ is the corresponding average of the original one-coordinate cost.
  It is nonnegative and continuous, with

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.2)")
 $ integral_X h_i thin d mu = C_j alpha_i \, wide mu \( "supp" h_i \) lt.eq alpha_i +
   2 delta_i . $ <eq:smoothed-support>]

For

$ d_i^(upright("sm")) \( x \) = C_j #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] s_i \( u_i \( x \) \) -
  u_i \( x \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, $

we have the exact measurable identity

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.3)")
 $ h_i \( x \) = C_j floor.l u_i \( x \) + alpha_i floor.r + d_i^(upright("sm")) \( x \) -
   d_i^(upright("sm")) \( sigma x \) . $ <eq:smoothing-coboundary>]

The support of $d_i^(upright("sm"))$ has measure at most $2 delta_i$.

Recall the shrinking arcs $O_j$, the integers $h_j^(*)$, and the continuous partition of unity $phi.alt_(j \, n)$,
  $0 lt.eq n lt.eq h_j^(*)$, prepared in the parameter construction,
  with $sigma^n \( "supp" phi.alt_(j \, n) \) subset O_j$. Define

$ h'_i \( x \) = sum_(n = 0)^(h_j^(*)) \( h_i phi.alt_(j \, n) \) \( sigma^(- n) x \) . $

This is continuous and nonnegative, supported in $O_j$, and has the same integral as $h_i$. Telescoping gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.4)")
 $ h'_i = h_i + d'_i - d'_i compose sigma \, wide d'_i \( x \) =
   sum_(n = 1)^(h_j^(*)) sum_(r = 1)^n \( h_i phi.alt_(j \, n) \) \( sigma^(- r) x \) . $ <eq:relocation-coboundary>]

Although this transfer contains a double sum, its support is contained in just $h_j^(*)$ translates of $"supp"
  h_i$, one for each possible $r$. Consequently

$ sum_i (mu { d_i^(upright("sm")) eq.not 0 } + mu { d'_i eq.not 0 }) lt.eq C sum_j frac(1 + h_j^(*), K_j) < oo . $

Thus each of the transfer sums has only finitely many nonzero terms almost everywhere,
  including at the translated point $sigma x$.

The sum $sum_i h'_i$ is locally finite on $X \\ { 0 }$ because its successive blocks are supported in $O_j arrow.b
  { 0 }$. It is therefore continuous there. Its integral is infinite:
  Tonelli’s theorem and $alpha_i > 1 \/ \( m_j K_j \)$ give

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.5)")
 $ integral_X sum_i h'_i thin d mu = sum_j C_j sum_(i in J_j) alpha_i gt.eq sum_j C_j \/ K_j = oo \,
   $ <eq:infinite-cost-integral>]

since $C_j gt.eq K_j^2$. Local finiteness alone need not force divergence at $0$;
  the following background supplies that remaining property.

=== 6.3 A background cost diverging at the zero <a-background-cost-diverging-at-the-zero>

Let $W_j = 2^(j + 1)$. Choose continuous functions $0 lt.eq v_j lt.eq 1$ with $v_j \( 0 \) = 1$,
  supported in closed arcs $I_j$ shrinking to ${ 0 }$. Choose these arcs small enough that $sigma I_j \, dots.h \,
  sigma^(W_j) I_j$ are pairwise disjoint and

$ sum_j W_j mu \( I_j \) < oo . $

This is possible because each finite set of distinct points $theta \, 2 theta \, dots.h \,
  W_j theta$ has positive minimum separation. For $x eq.not 0$, put

$ g_0 \( x \) = 1 + sum_j (v_j \( x \) - 1 / W_j sum_(n = 1)^(W_j) v_j \( sigma^(- n) x \)) . $

For each $j$, disjointness bounds the norm of the subtracted function by $1 \/ W_j$.
  These bounds sum to $1 \/ 2$, so the negative series converges uniformly.
  The positive series is locally finite away from $0$. Consequently $g_0$ is continuous there and $g_0 gt.eq
  1 \/ 2$. For every fixed integer $J$, the finite sum $sum_(j lt.eq J) v_j \( x \)$ tends to $J$ as $x arrow.r
  0$. The bounded negative series therefore implies $g_0 \( x \) arrow.r oo$ as $x arrow.r 0$.
  Its integral is finite, since $sum_j integral v_j thin d mu lt.eq
  sum_j mu \( I_j \) < oo$ and the negative series is uniformly bounded.

Define

$ b_0 \( x \) = sum_j 1 / W_j sum_(n = 1)^(W_j) sum_(r = 1)^n v_j \( sigma^(- r) x \) . $

The $j$th summand is supported in $union.big_(r = 1)^(W_j) sigma^r I_j$.
  The assumed summability makes this sum finite almost everywhere.
  On a conull set where the sums at $x$ and $sigma x$ are finite, telescoping shows

$ g_0 = 1 + b_0 compose sigma - b_0 . $

Now let

$ g \( x \) = g_0 \( x \) + sum_i h'_i \( x \) quad \( x eq.not 0 \) \, wide g \( 0 \) = oo \, wide b = b_0 -
  sum_i \( d_i^(upright("sm")) + d'_i \) . $

The preceding arguments prove that $g$ is nonnegative, finite and continuous off $0$, tends to infinity at $0$,
  and has infinite integral. The transfer $b$ is real and finite almost everywhere.
  Summing (6.3) and (6.4) is legitimate on the common conull set where the transfer sums and the original cost
  have finitely many nonzero terms. Together with the background identity this proves (6.1).

=== 6.4 Conclusion and spectral consequences <conclusion-and-spectral-consequences>

Define the function on the unit circle by

$ f \( e^(2 pi i x) \) = e^(- g \( x \)) \, wide e^(- oo) = 0 . $

It is continuous, is positive away from $1$, and vanishes at $1$.
  Its modulus is nonconstant and its zero set has Haar measure zero. Moreover,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.6)")
 $ integral_(bb(T)) log f thin d m = - oo . $ <eq:zero-log-integral>]

In particular, its Fuglede–Kadison determinant is zero #cite(label("fuglede-kadison")):
  the spectral distribution of $pi \( f \)$ is the Haar distribution of $f$,
  so the determinant definition is precisely the exponential of (6.6).
  Also $\| U pi \( f \) \| = pi \( f \)$, which gives the same determinant for $T_f = U pi \( f \)$.
  This conclusion uses the nonnegative integral calculation (6.5);
  it does not require the transfer $b$ to be integrable.

Choose a finite Borel version of $b$ and discard all integer translates of the null set where the coboundary
  identity fails. Let $H_b$ be multiplication on the $k$th fiber coordinate by $e^(b \( sigma^k x \))$,
  with its natural square-integrability domain. It is positive, self-adjoint, and injective.
  Its spectral projections are $pi$-images of spectral indicators of $e^b$, hence lie in $M$.
  Thus $H_b$ is affiliated with $M$. The coboundary identity gives the pointwise factor equality

$ e^(b \( sigma^(k + 1) x \)) e^(- g \( sigma^k x \)) = e^(- 1) f_(*) \( sigma^k x \) e^(b \( sigma^k x \)) . $

For every $zeta in "Dom" H_b$, the right-hand side defines the $k + 1$ coordinate of $e^(- 1) T_(*) H_b zeta$,
  a square-integrable vector because $T_(*)$ is bounded. Therefore

$ T_f zeta in "Dom" H_b \, wide H_b T_f zeta = e^(- 1) T_(*) H_b zeta . $

Lemma 21 sends any invariant projection of $T_f$ to an invariant projection of $e^(- 1)
  T_(*)$ having the same trace. Multiplication by the nonzero scalar $e^(- 1)$ does not change invariant ranges.
  Theorem 18 therefore excludes traces strictly between $0$ and $1$.
  This proves Theorem 1 for the arbitrary irrational $theta$ fixed at the start.

The same continuous weight has a stronger spectral property, which we record with an elementary proof.
  This is the zero-determinant case of #cite(label("zfs"), form: "prose") Theorem 2.3.
  We include the truncated-logarithm argument, which uses uniform averaging under irrational rotation.

#thm([Corollary 6.2.])[
  #emph[The constructed operator $T_f$ is nonzero and quasinilpotent.]
] <cor:quasinilpotent>

#proof[
  For $M > 0$, the function $ell_M \( x \) = log max \( f \( e^(2 pi i x) \) \, e^(- M) \)$ is continuous.
    Its integrals decrease to $- oo$ by (6.6). Irrational rotation averages of a continuous function converge
    uniformly to its integral: this follows for Fourier monomials from their geometric sums and then for
    continuous functions by uniform trigonometric approximation.
    Hence, for any $a > 0$, first choosing $M$ with $integral_X ell_M thin d mu < -
    2 a$ and then taking $n$ sufficiently large gives
  $ sup_(x in X) product_(k = 0)^(n - 1) f \( e^(2 pi i sigma^k x) \) lt.eq
    sup_(x in X) exp (sum_(k = 0)^(n - 1) ell_M \( sigma^k x \)) lt.eq e^(- a n) . $
  The expression on the left is $parallel T_f^n parallel$, because the power is a unitary $U^n$ times
    multiplication by that continuous product. The spectral-radius formula yields radius zero,
    since $a$ was arbitrary. Finally $T_f eq.not 0$ because $f > 0$ almost everywhere. ◻
]

== 7 A product-rotation realization <p:sec:product>

The circle proof replaces independent coordinates by divisible characters and ordered digits.
  On a countable product of circles, independence is available directly. This gives a different sampling result:
  prescribed triangular laws in any finite set of coordinates are weak limits of positive orbit-pair measures,
  with both marginal densities uniformly bounded in $L^2$. We prove that result and use it to construct a second
  concrete model for the convolution obstruction.

All notation in this appendix is local. Let

$ X = bb(T)^(bb(N)) \, wide bb(T) = bb(R) \/ bb(Z) \, $

with product Haar probability $mu$ and its compact metrizable product topology.
  Coordinates are represented in $\[ 0 \, 1 \)$, and $x lt.eq y$ means $x_i lt.eq y_i$ for every $i$.
  This order is Borel; its graph need not be closed at the circle cuts.
  For a sequence of angles $theta = \( theta_i \)$, write $sigma_theta x = x + theta$.
  On $cal(H) = L^2 \( X \, mu \; ell^2 \( bb(Z) \) \)$, put

$ \( pi \( a \) zeta \) \( x \)_k = a \( sigma_theta^k x \) zeta \( x \)_k \, wide \( U zeta \) \( x \) =
  S zeta \( x \) \, wide S e_k = e_(k + 1) \, $

and let $M = W^(*) \( U \, pi \( L^oo \( X \) \) \)$.

#thm([Theorem 7.1.])[
  (Product realization).  #emph[There are angles $0 < theta_i < 1$ and costs $c_i > 0$,
    with $sum_i theta_i < oo$ and $1 \, theta_1 \, theta_2 \, dots.h$ rationally independent,
    such that the measurable weight
$ f \( x \) = exp (- sum_(i gt.eq 1) c_i floor.l x_i + theta_i floor.r) $

 is positive almost everywhere and has $integral_X log f thin d mu = - oo$.
   The algebra $M$ is a type $upright("II")_1$ factor with separable predual, and $T =
   U pi \( f \)$ is nonzero and norm-quasinilpotent, with $Delta \( T \) = 0$.
   Every projection $p in M$ satisfying $\( 1 - p \) T p = 0$ is $0$ or $1$.]
  #emph[Moreover, $\| T \|$ has pure-point spectral type. The abelian algebra generated by any finite family of
    polar conjugates $U^n \| T \| U^(- n)$ is atomic, whereas $W^(*) \( U^n \| T \| U^(- n) : n in bb(Z) \)$ is
    diffuse.]
] <p:thm:model>

The last statement concerns spectral type: it does not require every point of the spectrum of $\|
  T \|$ to be an eigenvalue. In particular, zero can be a spectral accumulation point although $f >
  0$ almost everywhere. As in the principal construction, the absence of invariant projections refers to the
  specified containing factor.
  For instance, the subspaces $L^2 \( X \, mu \; overline("span") { e_k : k gt.eq m } \)$ are invariant under the
  represented forward weighted shift, but their constant-field projections do not satisfy the covariance required
  for membership in $M$.

The proof has three product-specific tasks. We first choose angles for which the finite-coordinate sampling
  theorem holds. We then pass the invariant projection identity from those measures to biased product couplings.
  Finally we verify the positive-event and tail-attenuation hypotheses of Lemma 17.
  The field decomposition, compact-set transfer, and scalar sampling and density estimates are the lemmas already
  proved in the main text; no part of this appendix is needed for the prescribed-angle theorem.

=== 7.1 Scales and independent angles <p:sec:scales>

We choose the finite polynomial accuracy before choosing each new block.
  This leaves enough freedom to obtain both the sampling estimates and the threshold crossings needed by Lemma 17.
  All choices in this subsection are independent of any invariant projection.

Partition $bb(N) = { 1 \, 2 \, dots.h }$ into consecutive finite blocks $J_j$ of length $m_j$, writing

$ d_0 = 0 \, wide d_j = sum_(s lt.eq j) m_s \, wide J_j = { d_(j - 1) + 1 \, dots.h \, d_j } . $

The cutoff $K_j$ will specify the finite coefficient window in the contradiction,
  while $D_j$ and $epsilon_j$ will give the coefficient bound and the product lower bound from Lemma 7.
  After these are fixed, $L_j$ specifies the required decay of the omitted terms;
  the logarithmic drop $C_j$ and the block length $m_j$ are chosen to achieve that decay while retaining uniform
  bounds on the coupling marginals.

We now choose these parameters. Put $kappa_j = 1 \/ \( 100 j \)$ and start with $K_1 = 100$ and $G_1 = 1$.
  At stage $j$, the numbers $K_j \, G_j$ and all parameters of earlier blocks have already been fixed.
  Make the following choices in the stated order.

+ Define
  $ D_j = exp (\( K_j + 1 \) sum_(s < j) C_s m_s) . $
  Apply Lemma 7 with $K = K_j$, $D = D_j$, and $delta = 1 \/ j$ to choose $epsilon_j > 0$.
    This is possible because $D_j gt.eq 1 gt.eq 1 \/ j$. Consequently,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.1)")
   $ max_(0 lt.eq n lt.eq 2 K_j) \| \( A B \)_(- n) \| gt.eq epsilon_j $ <p:eq:poly-lower>]
  whenever $A \, B$ are supported in $\[ - K_j \, K_j \]$, their coefficients have modulus at most $D_j$,
    and $\| A_0 \| \, \| B_0 \| gt.eq 1 \/ j$.
+ Choose $L_j gt.eq 1$, increasing with $j$, so large that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.2)")
   $ \( 2 K_j + 1 \) 3 exp \( - L_j \/ 10000 \) lt.eq epsilon_j / j . $ <p:eq:epsilon-choice>]
  Set $C_j = max { L_j \, K_j^2 }$, and choose a finite $G_(j + 1) gt.eq 1$ such that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.3)")
   $ kappa_(j + 1) sqrt(G_(j + 1)) gt.eq L_j . $ <p:eq:G-choice>]
+ Choose an integer $m = m_j$ sufficiently large, and set
  $ K_(j + 1) = ⌈m^(\( 3 \/ 4 \) m)⌉ \, $
  subject to
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.4)")
   $ m gt.eq max { K_j \, 2^j \, 4 G_j \, 100 } \, wide d_(j - 1) lt.eq m \/ 100 \,\
kappa_j m^(1 \/ 40) gt.eq L_j \, wide G_(j + 1) K_(j + 1) < m^(\( 4 \/ 5 \) m) . $ <p:eq:scale-conditions>]

Every choice is finite and feasible. Indeed, the first two steps do not depend on the new $m_j$.
  All but the final requirement in (7.4) are lower bounds on $m$.
  For the final one, the already fixed number $G_(j + 1)$ satisfies

$ frac(G_(j + 1) ceil.l m^(\( 3 \/ 4 \) m) ceil.r, m^(\( 4 \/ 5 \) m)) lt.eq 2 G_(j + 1) m^(- m \/ 20) arrow.r 0 . $

There is therefore no upper restriction conflicting with the lower bounds.

Write

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.5)")
 $ N_j = m_j K_j \, wide a_j = G_j \/ m_j \, wide b_j = m_j^(- 19 \/ 20) . $ <p:eq:N-and-widths>]

The construction gives the useful consequences

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.6)")
 $ m_j gt.eq 2^j \, wide d_j lt.eq 1.01 m_j \, wide N_j lt.eq m_j^2 \,\
K_(j + 1) > 2 K_j \, wide sum_(s > j) 1 / K_s lt.eq 2 / K_(j + 1) \, wide 1 / m_j lt.eq a_j \, b_j lt.eq 1 /
  4 . $ <p:eq:scale-consequences>]

For example, $m_j gt.eq 100$ and $m_j gt.eq K_j$ imply $K_(j + 1) gt.eq m_j^(\( 3 \/ 4 \) m_j) > 2 m_j gt.eq
  2 K_j$, and the reciprocal estimate follows by summing a geometric majorant.
  Also $m_(j + 1) gt.eq K_(j + 1) > m_j$, so each of $m_j \, K_j \, N_j$ is increasing.
  The upper bound for $a_j$ follows from $m_j gt.eq 4 G_j$; the other width bounds follow from $G_j gt.eq
  1$ and $m_j gt.eq 100$.

Use the coordinate order on $X$ defined above.

On a separate product probability space choose independent random angles $theta_i$,
  with $theta_i$ uniform on $\[ 1 \/ N_j \, 2 \/ N_j \]$ when $i in J_j$, and let

$ sigma_theta x = x + theta quad upright("in ") X . $

For every realization,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.7)")
 $ sum_(i = 1)^oo theta_i lt.eq 2 sum_(j = 1)^oo m_j / N_j = 2 sum_(j = 1)^oo 1 / K_j < oo . $ <p:eq:angle-summability>]

Almost surely $1 \, theta_1 \, theta_2 \, dots.h$ are rationally independent.
  To see this, multiply any proposed finite rational relation by a common denominator.
  If some angle has a nonzero integer coefficient, conditioning on all other angles leaves one prescribed value of
  an atomless random variable, an event of probability zero.
  A relation with no angle term cannot hold unless its constant term is zero.
  There are only countably many nontrivial finite integer relations.

For a rationally independent realization the rotation is ergodic and free. Indeed, the characters

$ x mapsto exp (2 pi i sum_i v_i x_i) \, wide v_i in bb(Z) \, quad v_i = 0 upright(" for all but finitely many ") i \, $

form an orthonormal basis of $L^2 \( mu \)$: finite-coordinate functions are dense,
  and the usual Fourier bases are complete on each finite torus.
  Translation multiplies this character by $exp \( 2 pi i sum_i v_i theta_i \)$,
  which equals $1$ only for the constant character. Hence the invariant $L^2$ functions are constant,
  which is ergodicity. Also $n theta_1 in.not bb(Z)$ for every $n in bb(Z) \\ { 0 }$,
  so $sigma_theta^n x eq.not x$ for every $x in X$.

#thm([Proposition 7.2.])[
  (Phase ranges).  #emph[Almost surely, for every sufficiently large $j$ the following hold simultaneously for all
    integers $k$ in the indicated ranges, with $m = m_j$:]
  + #emph[If $G_j K_j lt.eq \| k \| lt.eq N_j \/ 4$, then every $i in J_j$ satisfies
$ 1 - { k theta_i } in \[ a_j \, 1 - a_j \] . $

]
  + #emph[If $N_j \/ 4 lt.eq \| k \| lt.eq m^(\( 4 \/ 5 \) m)$, then at least $m \/ 20$ coordinates $i in J_j$ satisfy
$ 1 - { k theta_i } in \[ b_j \, 1 - b_j \] . $

]
  #emph[The first assertion in fact holds at every stage and for every realization of the angles in their
    prescribed intervals.]
] <p:prop:phases>

#proof[
  In the first range, put $t = \| k \| theta_i$. The angle bounds give
  $ a_j = G_j \/ m_j lt.eq t lt.eq 1 \/ 2 . $
  For $k > 0$ the threshold is $1 - t$, and for $k < 0$ it is $t$.
    Both belong to $\[ a_j \, 1 - a_j \]$ because $a_j lt.eq 1 \/ 4$.
  For the second range, the uniform law of $k theta_i$ on an interval of length $\| k \| \/ N_j$ has,
    after reduction modulo one, a density bounded by
  $ 1 + N_j \/ \| k \| lt.eq 5 . $
  This follows by counting the integer translates covering a point:
    the covering multiplicity of an interval of length $r$ is at most $r + 1$.
    The statement also applies to negative $k$ by reflection.
    Thus a given coordinate misses $\[ b_j \, 1 - b_j \]$ with probability at most $10 b_j$.
    If fewer than $m \/ 20$ coordinates satisfy the assertion,
    more than $19 m \/ 20$ coordinates miss the interval. Independence over $i in
    J_j$ and a union bound over the possible sets of misses show that, for a fixed $k$,
    this failure probability is at most $2^m \( 10 b_j \)^(\( 19 \/ 20 \) m)$ for large $m$.
    A further union bound over the at most $2 m^(\( 4 \/ 5 \) m)$ nonzero integers in the range gives
  $ bb(P) \( upright("failure at stage ") j \) lt.eq 2 m^(\( 4 \/ 5 \) m) 2^m \( 10 b_j \)^(\( 19 \/ 20 \) m) =
    exp (- 41 / 400 m log m + O \( m \)) . $
  These probabilities are summable because $m_j gt.eq 2^j$.
    The first Borel–Cantelli Lemma now gives the second assertion for all sufficiently large $j$,
    simultaneously for every integer in each stated range. ◻
]

We retain the random angles until the sampling construction below has supplied one further probability-one property.

=== 7.2 Sampling ordered orbit pairs <p:sec:sampling>

We next approximate prescribed changes in finitely many coordinates by positive orbit moves.
  Both endpoint distributions must remain controlled: this will allow the projection identity to pass from orbit
  pairs to the couplings used in the product construction. The scalar estimates of Lemma 6 apply coordinate by
  coordinate; we prove here the growing-product estimate and the ordered weak limit.

The coordinate order is only Borel in the product-circle topology:
  already in one circle, the ordered pairs $\( 1 \/ 2 \, 1 - 1 \/ n \)$ converge to the unordered pair
  $\( 1 \/ 2 \, 0 \)$. Thus the closed-relation hypothesis in the classical coupling theorem
  #cite(label("Edwards1978")) Corollary 3.2 does not apply directly.
  The result below also requires approximating measures supported on positive orbit pairs,
  with both marginal densities controlled; those are additional conclusions proved by the sampling construction.

For a finite set $I subset bb(N)$, define a finite Borel measure $lambda_I$ on $X times X$ as follows.
  In each coordinate $i in I$ use the unnormalized triangular measure

$ upright(bold(1))_({ 0 lt.eq x_i < y_i < 1 }) thin d x_i thin d y_i \, $

and in each other coordinate use the law of $\( x_i \, x_i \)$ for uniform $x_i in \[ 0 \, 1 \)$.
  Take the product over all coordinates. Equivalently, one can first normalize the finitely many triangular
  factors and then multiply the resulting probability measure by $2^(- \| I \|)$. In particular,

$ lambda_I \( X times X \) = 2^(- \| I \|) . $

Its two marginal densities relative to $mu$ are, respectively,
  $product_(i in I) \( 1 - x_i \)$ and $product_(i in I) y_i$; they are bounded by $1$.
  The measure is carried by $x lt.eq y$.

#thm([Proposition 7.3.])[
  (Ordered orbit-pair sampling).  #emph[With probability one in the choice of $theta$,
    the following holds for every finite $I subset bb(N)$. There are positive finite Borel measures $eta_(I \, l)$
    on $X times X$ converging weakly to $lambda_I$, each carried by ordered pairs of the form
$ \( x \, y \) = \( x \, sigma_theta^n x \) $

 with $n$ in a finite set of positive integers. Both marginals of $eta_(I \, l)$ have $L^2 \( mu \)$ densities,
   and their $L^2$ norms are bounded uniformly in $l$. The bound may depend on $I$ and on the fixed realization of
   $theta$. Weak convergence here means convergence against every continuous function on the compact space $X times X$.]
] <p:prop:sampling>

#proof[
  We give the construction and its estimates before choosing the probability-one event.
    The notation $O \( dot.op \)$ in this proof has absolute constants, uniform in the coordinate,
    the selected powers, and the interval endpoints involved in a rectangle test.
  We average many separated orbit powers so that both marginal densities approach those of an ideal model with
    independent uniform phases. The coordinate weights impose triangular laws on $I$ and concentrate near the
    diagonal elsewhere, while their marginal bounds remain uniform in the growing product.
    A final restriction to ordered tail coordinates removes asymptotically negligible mass.
  ===== 7.2.1 Separated powers and phase discrepancy <separated-powers-and-phase-discrepancy>
  Take $l$ sufficiently large that $m_l gt.eq 200$, as required by the scalar lemma.
    At stage $l$ write $m = m_l$, $d = d_l$, and set
  $ Q = m^(\( 7 \/ 10 \) m) \, wide H = m^300 \, wide R = N_l m^1000 . $
  For all sufficiently large $l$ we can choose, deterministically,
    a set $cal(N)_l$ of integers in $\[ Q \/ 2 \, Q \]$ such that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.8)")
   $ P_l := \| cal(N)_l \| gt.eq m^(0.69 m) \,\
\| v n + v' n' \| > R quad
  vec(n \, n' in cal(N)_l \, med n eq.not n' \,, v \, v' in bb(Z) \, med \| v \| \, \| v' \| lt.eq H \, med \(
  v \, v' \) eq.not \( 0 \, 0 \)) . $ <p:eq:sampling-separation>]
  Indeed, given a previously selected $n$, a fixed frequency pair with $v' eq.not
    0$ forbids an interval of possible $n'$ of length $2 R \/ \| v' \| lt.eq 2 R$, hence at most $2 R +
    2$ integers. There are $O \( H^2 \)$ frequency pairs. Since $N_l lt.eq m^2$,
    each selection therefore excludes at most $O \( m^1602 \)$ integers.
    When $v' = 0$ and $v eq.not 0$, the inequality is automatic because $n gt.eq Q \/ 2 > R$ eventually; likewise,
    a zero first frequency creates no obstruction. The constraints are symmetric in the selected powers,
    since the full set of frequency pairs is used. Greedy selection from the $asymp
    Q$ available integers yields at least $c Q \/ m^1602 gt.eq m^(0.69 m)$ choices for large $m$.
  For a coordinate $i in J_s$ with $s lt.eq l$, the angle $theta_i$ is uniform on $\[ 1 \/ N_s \, 2 \/ N_s \]$.
    Apply the separated-phase part of Lemma 6 with
  $ N_0 = N_s lt.eq N_l = N lt.eq m^2 . $
  The powers just selected satisfy its separation hypothesis.
    Thus $t_n = { n theta_i }$ has a density $phi_(i \, n)$ satisfying
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.9)")
   $ parallel phi_(i \, n) - 1 parallel_oo lt.eq N_s \/ n lt.eq N_l \/ n . $ <p:eq:single-phase-density>]
  In particular, that density is at most two for large $l$.
    For distinct selected powers and functions $g \, g'$ of bounded variation, the same lemma gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.10)")
   $ lr(|bb(E)_theta g \( t_n \) g' \( t_(n') \) - (integral_0^1 g) (integral_0^1 g')|) & lt.eq
     c m^(- 80) \( parallel g parallel_oo + "Var" g \) \( parallel g' parallel_oo + "Var" g' \) .
     $ <p:eq:bv-discrepancy>]
  No independence between $t_n$ and $t_(n')$ is asserted. The imported estimate uses their Fourier coefficients
    and one-dimensional marginal densities, and so applies even when their joint law is singular.
    We shall also use the direct consequence of (7.9)
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.11)")
   $ lr(|bb(E)_theta g \( t_n \) - integral_0^1 g \( t \) thin d t|) lt.eq N_l /
     n integral_0^1 \| g \( t \) \| thin d t . $ <p:eq:single-phase-test>]
  ===== 7.2.2 The weighted measures and both marginal orientations <the-weighted-measures-and-both-marginal-orientations>
  Fix a finite $I$ and restrict to sufficiently large $l$ that $I subset { 1 \, dots.h \, d }$. Set
  $ h = m^(- 3 \/ 5) \, wide Delta \( s \) = min \( s \, 1 - s \) \, wide alpha = m^(- 2) . $
  For $0 lt.eq s \, t < 1$ define
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.12)")
   $ w_i \( s \, t \) =
     cases(delim: "{", upright(bold(1))_({ 0 < t < 1 - s }) \, & i in I \,, upright(bold(1))_({ Delta \( s \)
     gt.eq alpha }) frac(upright(bold(1))_({ 0 < t < h Delta \( s \) }), h Delta \( s \)) \, & i in.not I .)
     $ <p:eq:sampling-weights>]
  The second line is defined to be zero if $Delta \( s \) < alpha$.
    Let $tilde(eta)_(I \, l)$ be the average, over $n in cal(N)_l$,
    of the push-forward under $x mapsto \( x \, sigma_theta^n x \)$ of
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.13)")
   $ W_n \( x \) thin d mu \( x \) \, wide W_n \( x \) = product_(i lt.eq d) w_i \( x_i \, { n theta_i } \) .
     $ <p:eq:untrimmed-weight>]
  Each weight is bounded for fixed $l$, so these are finite positive measures.
    They are carried by pairs satisfying $x_i < y_i$ for $i lt.eq d$, since $h Delta \( x_i \) lt.eq
    h \( 1 - x_i \) < 1 - x_i$ and $h < 1$.
  We will treat the marginals both without a test and after restriction to a cylinder rectangle
  $ cal(R) = { \( x \, y \) : x_i in E_i^x \, med y_i in E_i^y upright(" for every ") i } \, $
  where the $E_i^x \, E_i^y$ are intervals with rational endpoints and all but finitely many are the whole circle.
    Take $l$ large enough that all tested coordinates are at most $d$.
    A missing test is represented by using the whole circle for every interval.
    For the first and second marginal, respectively, define
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.14)")
   $ u_i^(\( 1 \)) \( s \, t \) & = w_i \( s \, t \) upright(bold(1))_(E_i^x) \( s \)
     upright(bold(1))_(E_i^y) \( { s + t } \) \,\
u_i^(\( 2 \)) \( s \, t \) & = w_i \( { s - t } \, t \) upright(bold(1))_(E_i^x) \( { s - t } \)
  upright(bold(1))_(E_i^y) \( s \) . $ <p:eq:oriented-weights>]
  Changing variables $y = sigma_theta^n x$ preserves Haar measure.
    It follows that the two tested marginal densities are exactly the averages of the products of the
    corresponding $u_i^(\( r \))$. There is no factor from coordinates past $d$, even in the second orientation,
    since their unweighted Haar law is translation invariant.
  We check the extra factors introduced by triangular laws and interval tests.
    Write $u_i$ for either orientation and put
  $ macron(u)_i \( s \) = integral_0^1 u_i \( s \, t \) thin d t \, wide A_i =
    integral_0^1 macron(u)_i \( s \)^2 thin d s . $
  Uniformly in $s$, the coordinate, and the test intervals, we have
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.15)")
   $ parallel u_i \( s \, dot.op \) parallel_oo + "Var"_t u_i \( s \, dot.op \) = O \( m^(13 \/ 5) \) =
     O \( m^10 \) . $ <p:eq:weight-variation>]
  For an untested triangular factor, the bound is $O \( 1 \)$:
    in the first orientation its support in $t$ is $0 < t < 1 - s$, and in the second it is $0 < t lt.eq s$.
    The possible value at $t = s$ affects no integral. For the untested near-diagonal factors,
    Lemma 6 gives the bound $O \( m^(13 \/ 5) \)$. To include the tests,
    in either orientation the extra factor is a constant in ${ 0 \,
    1 }$ times an interval indicator translated or reflected modulo one.
    Its supremum is at most one and its variation is bounded by an absolute constant. The inequality
  $ "Var" \( a b \) lt.eq parallel a parallel_oo "Var" \( b \) + parallel b parallel_oo "Var" \( a \) $
  therefore proves (7.15), including all jumps at the test endpoints.
  The moment bounds needed below are
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.16)")
   $ 0 lt.eq macron(u)_i \( s \) lt.eq 2 \, wide 0 lt.eq A_i lt.eq 1 + O \( h^2 \) \, wide
     integral_0^1 integral_0^1 u_i \( s \, t \)^2 thin d s thin d t =
     O \( h^(- 1) log m \) . $ <p:eq:one-coordinate-moments>]
  For an untested triangular factor the two averages are exactly $1 - s$ and $s$, respectively.
    In either orientation its total integral and squared integral are $1 \/ 2$, while $A_i = 1 \/ 3$.
    For an untested near-diagonal factor, Lemma 6 gives
  $ parallel macron(u)_i parallel_oo lt.eq 2 \, wide A_i lt.eq 1 + frac(h^2, \( 1 - h \)^2) \, wide
    integral.double u_i \( s \, t \)^2 thin d s thin d t = 2 / h log frac(1, 2 alpha) . $
  Since tests only decrease the nonnegative factors and their averages,
    these statements imply (7.16) for every tested factor. Their actual masses need not agree; in particular,
    the near-diagonal integral identities are not being imposed on the triangular factors or on the tested
    kernels. The quadratic error $O \( h^2 \)$ is what keeps the product of the moment bounds controlled as the
    number of coordinates grows.
  ===== 7.2.3 The estimate in the growing product dimension <the-estimate-in-the-growing-product-dimension>
  For either fixed orientation and a fixed rectangle test or no test, write
  $ q_n \( z \) & = product_(i lt.eq d) u_i \( z_i \, { n theta_i } \) \, & Z_l \( z \) & = 1 /
    P_l sum_(n in cal(N)_l) q_n \( z \) \,\
Z_l^0 \( z \) & = product_(i lt.eq d) macron(u)_i \( z_i \) \, & A & = product_(i lt.eq d) A_i =
  parallel Z_l^0 parallel_2^2 . $
  We prove the estimate
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.17)")
   $ bb(E)_theta parallel Z_l - Z_l^0 parallel_(L^2 \( mu \))^2 lt.eq O \( d m^(- 40) \) +
     frac(\( c h^(- 1) log m \)^d, P_l) . $ <p:eq:sampling-L2>]
  The independence used here is independence across coordinates $theta_i$.
    In a single coordinate two phases may still be dependent, as already accounted for in (7.10).
  For distinct $n \, n'$, Tonelli’s Theorem, product Haar measure,
    and coordinate independence yield the exact factorization
  $ bb(E)_theta chevron.l q_n \, q_(n') chevron.r = product_(i lt.eq d) integral_0^1 bb(E)_theta
    #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] u_i \( s \, { n theta_i } \) u_i \( s \, { n' theta_i } \)
    #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]] thin d s . $
  By (7.15) and (7.10), its $i$th factor is $A_i + e_i$ with $\| e_i \| lt.eq c m^(- 50)$. Similarly,
  $ bb(E)_theta chevron.l q_n \, Z_l^0 chevron.r = product_(i lt.eq d) integral_0^1 macron(u)_i \( s \)
    bb(E)_theta u_i \( s \, { n theta_i } \) thin d s . $
  Here (7.11) and $macron(u)_i lt.eq 2$ bound the error in each factor from $A_i$ by $4 N_l \/ n =
    O \( m^(- 50) \)$. Thus both sorts of factors have the same stated error bound.
  The product errors do not require any lower bound on $A_i$. Telescoping gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.18)")
   $ lr(|product_(i lt.eq d) \( A_i + e_i \) - product_(i lt.eq d) A_i|) lt.eq
     c d m^(- 50) \( 1 + c h^2 + c m^(- 50) \)^(d - 1) = O \( d m^(- 50) \) \, $ <p:eq:sampling-product-error>]
  because (7.6) gives $d lt.eq 1.01 m$, and hence $d h^2 lt.eq 1.01 m^(- 1 \/ 5)$.
    In particular, $A lt.eq \( 1 + c h^2 \)^d = O \( 1 \)$.
  For a self-pairing, use only the one-phase density bound at most $2$ and (7.16):
  $ bb(E)_theta parallel q_n parallel_2^2 = product_(i lt.eq d) integral_0^1 bb(E)_theta
    u_i \( s \, { n theta_i } \)^2 thin d s lt.eq \( c h^(- 1) log m \)^d . $
  To see the cancellation of all other main terms explicitly, expand the squared norm.
    The distinct-pair terms have total averaging weight $1 - 1 \/ P_l$ and main term $\( 1 - 1 \/ P_l \) A$.
    The cross terms with $Z_l^0$ have main term $- 2 A$, and the final squared norm of $Z_l^0$ is $A$.
    Their sum is $- A \/ P_l lt.eq 0$. By (7.18), their remaining contribution has absolute value
    $O \( d m^(- 50) \)$. The self-pairings have total weight $1 \/ P_l$.
    Dropping $- A \/ P_l$ proves the slightly weaker bound (7.17).
  This estimate is summable in $l$. Indeed,
  $ log_m \( c h^(- 1) log m \)^d lt.eq 1.01 m (3 / 5 + log_m \( c log m \)) lt.eq 0.65 m $
  for large $m$, whereas $P_l gt.eq m^(0.69 m)$. Thus
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.19)")
   $ bb(E)_theta parallel Z_l - Z_l^0 parallel_2^2 lt.eq O \( m^(- 39) \) + m^(- 0.04 m) \, $ <p:eq:sampling-summable>]
  whose sum is finite by $m_l gt.eq 2^l$.
  ===== 7.2.4 One probability-one event and the weak limit <one-probability-one-event-and-the-weak-limit>
  For each fixed finite $I$, orientation, and rational cylinder rectangle or absent test,
    Tonelli’s Theorem applied to (7.19) shows that
  $ sum_l parallel Z_l - Z_l^0 parallel_2^2 < oo quad upright("almost surely") \, $
  where only the sufficiently large admissible stages are included.
    In particular, $parallel Z_l - Z_l^0 parallel_2 arrow.r 0$.
    Finite subsets of $bb(N)$ form a countable family, as do finite cylinder rectangles with rational endpoints,
    and there are only two orientations. Intersect these countably many probability-one events.
    For the remainder of the proof take any $theta$ in this intersection.
  Without a test, $Z_l$ is a marginal density of $tilde(eta)_(I \, l)$.
    Since $parallel Z_l^0 parallel_2^2 = A = O \( 1 \)$, the $L^2$ convergence just proved bounds both marginal
    norms uniformly in $l$. We may omit finitely many initial stages; alternatively,
    each of their densities is bounded for fixed $l$, so they can be included by enlarging the bound.
    The total masses of $tilde(eta)_(I \, l)$ are consequently uniformly bounded.
  For a fixed cylinder rectangle $cal(R)$, using the first orientation with its test shows that
  $ lr(|tilde(eta)_(I \, l) \( cal(R) \) - integral_X Z_l^0 thin d mu|) lt.eq parallel Z_l -
    Z_l^0 parallel_2 arrow.r 0 . $
  The ideal integral has a direct product interpretation on the first $d$ coordinates.
    For $i in I$, integration of $w_i \( x_i \, t \)$ in $x_i \,
    t$ gives exactly the triangular measure under $y_i = x_i + t$.
    For $i in.not I$, first take uniform $x_i$, discard $Delta \( x_i \) < alpha$, and, otherwise,
    take $t$ uniform in $\( 0 \, h Delta \( x_i \) \)$, setting $y_i = x_i + t$.
    Before the discard this is a probability law (the value at the two endpoint values of $x_i$ may be defined
    arbitrarily), and $\| y_i - x_i \| lt.eq h \/ 2$. The total loss caused by all cutoffs is at most
  $ 2 d alpha = 2 d m^(- 2) arrow.r 0 \, $
  by the union bound, with the finite triangular factors having mass at most one.
    Each fixed tested coordinate outside $I$ therefore converges to its uniform diagonal law.
    More explicitly, outside an $h$-neighborhood of a tested interval’s endpoints,
    membership of $y_i$ in that interval agrees with membership of $x_i$;
    the neighborhood has Lebesgue measure $O \( h \)$. Since a fixed rectangle tests only finitely many
    coordinates, all such errors vanish. Untested coordinates contribute only their masses,
    and the total cutoff estimate controls their product even as $d$ increases. It follows that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.20)")
   $ tilde(eta)_(I \, l) \( cal(R) \) arrow.r lambda_I \( cal(R) \) . $ <p:eq:sampling-rectangle-limit>]
  These limits imply weak convergence on $X times X$. Here are the topological details.
    The boundaries of the interval rectangles have zero $lambda_I$ measure,
    since every single-coordinate marginal of $lambda_I$ is absolutely continuous.
    Every continuous function on the compact product can be uniformly approximated by a continuous function of
    finitely many coordinates: in a summable product metric, replace the tail coordinates by fixed points and use
    uniform continuity. On the resulting finite torus, a sufficiently fine rational grid gives a step function,
    a finite linear combination of rational rectangle indicators, with arbitrarily small uniform error.
    Thus (7.20) and the uniform bound on total masses give convergence of integrals for every continuous function.
    In particular,
  $ tilde(eta)_(I \, l) med arrow.r.double med lambda_I . $
  ===== 7.2.5 Trimming the tail to impose order everywhere <trimming-the-tail-to-impose-order-everywhere>
  The untrimmed measures now have the required weak limit and both marginal bounds.
    It remains to impose order past the first $d$ coordinates.
    Define $eta_(I \, l)$ by restricting $tilde(eta)_(I \, l)$ to the Borel set where $x_i lt.eq
    y_i$ also for every $i > d$. For a fixed selected $n$, the weight $W_n$ depends only on the first $d$
    coordinates, so the tail $x$-coordinates remain independent uniform coordinates under that summand,
    apart from multiplication by its total mass. For a uniform tail coordinate,
    the probability that $x_i > { x_i + n theta_i }$ is exactly ${ n theta_i } lt.eq n theta_i$.
    Consequently, uniformly over $n in cal(N)_l$, the relative mass discarded from this summand is at most
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.21)")
   $ sum_(i > d) n theta_i & lt.eq 2 m^(\( 7 \/ 10 \) m) sum_(s > l) 1 / K_s\
 & lt.eq frac(4 m^(\( 7 \/ 10 \) m), K_(l + 1)) lt.eq 4 m^(- m \/ 20) arrow.r 0 . $ <p:eq:sampling-tail-trim>]
  The union bound is valid for the countable tail by increasing finite unions.
    More precisely, the discarded mass of a summand equals its total mass times the Haar probability of its tail
    failure event, because the weight and that event depend on disjoint coordinates.
    This statement also holds for a summand of mass zero. Averaging over $n$ shows that the total discarded mass
    is bounded by (7.21) times the uniformly bounded untrimmed mass.
    Hence the difference of the two measures tends to zero in total variation and $eta_(I \, l) arrow.r.double
    lambda_I$.
  Each trimmed marginal is a submeasure of the corresponding untrimmed marginal.
    Its Radon–Nikodym density is therefore nonnegative and bounded almost everywhere by the untrimmed density,
    so its $L^2$ norm cannot increase. Trimming preserves the finite set of positive orbit powers and now imposes
    order in every coordinate. All conclusions hold on the single probability-one event already constructed,
    for every finite $I$, proving the Proposition. ◻
]

We now fix one realization $theta$ for which rational independence, Proposition 24, and Proposition 25 all hold,
  and henceforth write $sigma = sigma_theta$. The scales and this rotation are fixed before any candidate
  invariant projection is introduced. For this fixed rotation,
  each sampling sequence has actual weak convergence against every continuous function;
  its validity is no longer restricted to the countable tests used to obtain the probability-one event.
  Thus later choices of continuous tests or compact sets depending on a candidate projection require no further
  intersection of events in the angle probability space.

=== 7.3 The product factor and its weighted shift <p:sec:operator>

We now turn the fixed rotation into an operator in a finite factor.
  The goal is to express invariance as an identity on ordered pairs, where the sampling result can act.
  Use the rotation $sigma$ fixed at the end of Section 7.2, and write $cal(K) = ell^2 \( bb(Z) \)$.
  The operators $U \, pi \( g \)$ and their generated algebra $M$ are those defined at the beginning of the
  appendix. This is the usual regular representation of a crossed product by a measure-preserving transformation
  #cite(label("AnantharamanPopa")) §1.4 and Proposition 1.4.5;
  the class of operators $U pi \( f \)$ is also considered in #cite(label("dykema-schultz")) Section 5.
  For completeness, we verify the properties of $M$ needed here.

#thm([Lemma 7.4.])[
  #emph[The algebra $M$ is a factor of type $upright("II")_1$ with separable predual.
    Its elements are essentially bounded measurable fields $H \( x \) in cal(B) \( cal(K) \)$ satisfying
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.22)")
 $ H \( sigma x \) = S^(- 1) H \( x \) S quad upright("almost everywhere") \, $ <p:eq:covariance>]

 and its faithful normal tracial state is
$ tau \( H \) = integral_X H \( x \)_(0 \, 0) thin d mu \( x \) . $

]
] <p:lem:factor>

#proof[
  Each generator commutes with scalar multiplication by any function of $x$.
    It also commutes with the unitary $\( J zeta \) \( x \) = S zeta \( sigma x \)$.
    These commutation relations therefore hold for every $H in M$.
    Lemma 9 therefore represents $H$ by an essentially bounded measurable field $H \( x \)$,
    with products and adjoints computed fiberwise. Commutation with $J$ gives (7.22).
  Conversely, let $H$ be an essentially bounded measurable field satisfying (7.22).
    All its integer-iterate covariance identities hold on one conull set.
    Put $h_r \( x \) = H \( x \)_(r \, 0)$ for $r in bb(Z)$. Then
  $ H \( x \)_(k + r \, k) = h_r \( sigma^k x \) \, $
  so the $r$th matrix diagonal of $H$ is $U^r pi \( h_r \) in M$.
    To recover $H$ from these diagonals, use the constant-field unitaries $D_t e_k = e^(2 pi i k t) e_k$,
    $t in bb(T)$. The strong integral
  $ H_N = integral_0^1 cal(F)_N \( t \) D_t H D_t^(*) thin d t =
    sum_(\| r \| < N) (1 - frac(\| r \|, N)) U^r pi \( h_r \) $
  belongs to $M$, where $cal(F)_N$ is the Fejér kernel. The map $t mapsto D_t H
    D_t^(*)$ is strongly continuous and uniformly bounded. The positivity,
    unit integral and concentration at zero of $cal(F)_N$ imply $H_N arrow.r H$ strongly.
    The strong closure of $M$ therefore proves the converse field characterization.
  The displayed state is the vector state associated with the constant field $e_0$, hence is normal.
    If $H gt.eq 0$ and $tau \( H \) = 0$, its $00$ entry vanishes almost everywhere.
    Covariance then forces every diagonal entry to vanish almost everywhere.
    Positivity, or the inequality $\| H_(k \, l) \|^2 lt.eq H_(k \, k) H_(l \, l)$, implies $H = 0$.
    Thus the state is faithful. For $H \, H' in M$, covariance and the change of variable $z = sigma^r x$ give
  $ integral_X H \( x \)_(0 \, r) H' \( x \)_(r \, 0) thin d mu \( x \) =
    integral_X H' \( z \)_(0 \, - r) H \( z \)_(- r \, 0) thin d mu \( z \) . $
  The sums over $r$ are absolutely integrable by the row and column $ell^2$ bounds and Cauchy–Schwarz.
    Summing proves $tau \( H H' \) = tau \( H' H \)$.
  The rotation $sigma$ is ergodic by Section 7.1. If $H$ is central,
    it commutes with $pi \( x mapsto e^(2 pi i x_1) \)$. In each fiber this last operator has distinct diagonal
    entries, because $theta_1$ is irrational; hence $H \( x \)$ is diagonal.
    Commutation with $U$ makes all its diagonal entries equal.
    Equation (7.22) and ergodicity make their common value constant almost everywhere.
    The center of $M$ is therefore scalar. The algebra contains the infinite-dimensional abelian algebra
    $pi \( L^oo \( X \) \)$, so this finite factor is not a matrix algebra and is of type $upright("II")_1$.
    Finally, the predual of a von Neumann algebra on a separable Hilbert space is a quotient of the separable
    trace class, and is separable. ◻
]

==== Weights and cocycles <weights-and-cocycles>

Using representatives $x_i in \[ 0 \, 1 \)$ for the circle coordinates, put

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.23)")
 $ f \( x \) = exp #h(-0.16667em) (- sum_(j gt.eq 1) C_j sum_(i in J_j) floor.l x_i + theta_i floor.r) \, wide T =
   U pi \( f \) . $ <p:eq:weights>]

Since $sum_i theta_i lt.eq 2 sum_j K_j^(- 1) < oo$, the first Borel–Cantelli Lemma shows that only finitely many
  terms in this sum are nonzero almost everywhere. Thus $0 < f lt.eq
  1$ almost everywhere and $T$ is a nonzero bounded operator. For every $k in bb(Z)$ define

$ F_k \( x \) = exp #h(-0.16667em) (- sum_(j gt.eq 1) C_j sum_(i in J_j) floor.l x_i + k theta_i floor.r) . $

For a fixed $k$, all sufficiently late coordinates have $\| k \| theta_i < 1$,
  and the probability that their floor is nonzero is $\| k \| theta_i$.
  Borel–Cantelli again proves that $F_k$ is positive and finite almost everywhere.
  Taking a countable intersection and all its $sigma$-translates gives an invariant Borel set $X_0$ of full
  measure on which all these formulas hold and

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.24)")
 $ F_0 = 1 \, wide F_(k + n) \( x \) = F_n \( sigma^k x \) F_k \( x \) quad \( k \, n in bb(Z) \) . $ <p:eq:cocycle>]

Indeed, the identity follows coordinatewise from $floor.l x_i + \( k + n \) theta_i floor.r = floor.l x_i +
  k theta_i floor.r + floor.l { x_i + k theta_i } + n theta_i floor.r$.
  Extend each $F_k$ to be positive and finite on $X \\ X_0$; its values there will never affect an integral.

For $x \, y in X_0$ with $x lt.eq y$ coordinatewise, set

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.25)")
 $ R_(x \, y) \( k \) = frac(F_k \( y \), F_k \( x \)) lt.eq 1 \, wide k in bb(Z) . $ <p:eq:ratio>]

The inequality holds for negative as well as positive $k$, since the floor function is nondecreasing.
  More precisely, each coordinate $i in J_s$ with

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.26)")
 $ x_i < 1 - { k theta_i } lt.eq y_i $ <p:eq:threshold-crossing>]

contributes a factor $e^(- C_s)$ to this ratio. All other coordinates contribute either $1$ or another such factor.

#thm([Lemma 7.5.])[
  #emph[The weight satisfies $integral_X log f thin d mu = - oo$ and $Delta \( T \) = 0$.
    The operator $T$ is nonzero and norm-quasinilpotent: $lim_(n arrow.r oo) parallel T^n parallel^(1 \/ n) = 0$.]
] <p:lem:quasinilpotent>

#proof[
  For $n gt.eq 1$, direct multiplication and (7.24) give $T^n = U^n pi \( F_n \)$.
    All the floors defining $F_n$ are nonnegative. For each fixed $q$, the estimate $floor.l x_i +
    n theta_i floor.r gt.eq n theta_i - 1$ on the first $q$ blocks therefore gives
  $ limsup_(n arrow.r oo) parallel T^n parallel^(1 \/ n) lt.eq
    exp #h(-0.16667em) (- sum_(s lt.eq q) C_s sum_(i in J_s) theta_i) lt.eq
    exp #h(-0.16667em) (- sum_(s lt.eq q) C_s / K_s) . $
  Since $C_s gt.eq K_s^2$, the last expression tends to zero as $q arrow.r oo$.
    Also, Tonelli’s Theorem and the same angle bounds give
  $ - integral_X log f thin d mu = sum_s C_s sum_(i in J_s) theta_i gt.eq sum_s C_s / K_s = oo . $
  Since $\| T \| = pi \( f \)$ and the trace on $pi \( L^oo \( X \) \)$ is integration against $mu$,
    the determinant formula gives $Delta \( T \) = 0$. Positivity of $f$ almost everywhere ensures $T eq.not 0$. ◻
]

The uniform norm estimate also gives $sigma \( T \) = { 0 }$ and Brown measure $delta_0$.

==== From orbit identities to triangular laws <from-orbit-identities-to-triangular-laws>

The sampled measures now allow us to use Lemma 11. The order supplies its uniform cap,
  and the two marginal $L^2$ bounds supply uniform absolute continuity.

#thm([Proposition 7.6.])[
  #emph[Suppose $p in M$ is a projection and $\( 1 - p \) T p = 0$. For every finite $I subset bb(N)$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.27)")
 $ \( 1 - p \( y \) \) "diag"_(k in bb(Z)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] R_(x \, y) \( k \)
   #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] p \( x \) = 0 quad upright("for ") lambda_I
   upright("-almost every ") \( x \, y \) . $ <p:eq:pair-identity>]

]
] <p:prop:pair-identity>

#proof[
  Fix this $p$ and a Borel version of its projection field.
    We may shrink $X_0$ to a common invariant Borel set of full measure on which covariance,
    the cocycle identities, and all the following countably many power identities hold.
    Invariance of $"ran" p \( x \)$ under $T \( x \)$ gives invariance under $T \( x \)^n$, for each $n gt.eq 1$.
    Applying $S^(- n)$ and using covariance yields
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.28)")
   $ \( 1 - p \( sigma^n x \) \) "diag"_(k in bb(Z)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] F_n \( sigma^k x \)
     #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] p \( x \) = 0 . $ <p:eq:orbit-inclusion>]
  Division by $F_n \( x \) > 0$ changes the diagonal entries to $F_k \( sigma^n x \) \/ F_k \( x \)$ by (7.24).
    Proposition 25 gives ordered orbit-pair measures converging to $lambda_I$ with uniformly $L^2$-bounded
    marginal densities. Their first marginals are absolutely continuous,
    so the countable collection of orbit identities holds almost everywhere on each approximant.
    The marginals of $lambda_I$ are absolutely continuous. The uniform $L^2$ bounds give uniform absolute
    continuity by Cauchy–Schwarz. Orderedness and (7.25) give the common cap $B = 1$ on every ratio,
    under both the approximants and the limit. Lemma 11 now proves (7.27). ◻
]

For later use, the common strict-diagonal lemma applies here:
  the base is ergodic, the integral trace is faithful, and $f > 0$ almost everywhere.
  Thus, for any projection $p in M$ with $0 < p < 1$ and $\( 1 - p \) T p = 0$, Lemma 10 gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.29)")
 $ 0 < r \( x \) := p \( x \)_(0 \, 0) < 1 quad upright("for almost every ") x . $ <p:eq:strict-diagonal>]

=== 7.4 Biased couplings of product coordinates <p:sec:couplings>

The measures $lambda_I$ give the pair identity when only finitely many coordinates are replaced.
  We now construct ordered probability laws that give every block opportunities to cross its thresholds.
  Their endpoint densities remain uniformly bounded in $L^2 \( mu \)$,
  and the crossing estimates remain valid after either entire endpoint is fixed.
  These are the properties needed for the convolution obstruction.

An ordered coupling with both marginals equal to Haar measure would give $x = y$ almost surely:
  in every coordinate, $y_i - x_i gt.eq 0$ and $bb(E) \( y_i - x_i \) = 0$.
  We therefore allow a controlled change in the endpoint marginals. Fix a block index $j gt.eq 1$.
  For $i in J_s$, write $m = m_s$ and set

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.30)")
 $ rho_s \( w \) = kappa_s sqrt(w \/ m) \, wide w = a_s \, b_s . $ <p:eq:rho-definition>]

Choose the coordinate pair $\( x_i \, y_i \)$ by the following mixture:

- For each of the two labelled widths $w = a_s \, b_s$, include a top component of probability $rho_s \( w \)$.
  In this component $x_i$ and $y_i$ are independent uniforms on $\[ 0 \, 1 - w \/ 2 \)$ and
  $\[ 1 - w \/ 2 \, 1 \)$, respectively.
- For each labelled width $w = a_s \, b_s$, include a bottom component of probability $rho_s \( w \)$,
  with independent uniforms on $\[ 0 \, w \/ 2 \)$ and $\[ w \/ 2 \, 1 \)$, respectively.
- If $s = j$, include a tagged component of probability $1 \/ m$,
  with independent uniforms on $\[ 0 \, 1 \/ m \)$ and $\[ 1 - 1 \/ m \, 1 \)$, respectively.
- With the remaining probability
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.31)")
   $ q_(j \, s) = 1 - 2 rho_s \( a_s \) - 2 rho_s \( b_s \) - upright(bold(1))_({ s = j }) \/ m \,
     $ <p:eq:diagonal-probability>]
  choose $x_i = y_i$ uniformly in $\[ 0 \, 1 \)$.

The width labels remain distinct even if their numerical values agree. The scale bounds imply

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.32)")
 $ 1 / m lt.eq a_s \, b_s lt.eq 1 / 4 \, wide frac(rho_s \( w \), w) = kappa_s / sqrt(m w) lt.eq kappa_s lt.eq 1 /
   100 . $ <p:eq:width-bounds>]

Since $m gt.eq 100$, it follows that $q_(j \, s) gt.eq 1 - kappa_s - 1 \/ m gt.eq 0.98$.

Make the choices independently over all coordinates. More precisely,
  let $\( Omega_j \, bb(P)_j \)$ be the countable product of the spaces carrying the mixture label and its
  coordinate pair. Let $x \, y : Omega_j arrow.r X$ be the endpoint maps,
  and let $nu_j$ be the law of $\( x \, y \)$ on $X^2$. The labels, including the tags,
  are thus retained as events on $Omega_j$. These are the actual product coordinates of the endpoints.
  Each component is supported on $x_i lt.eq y_i$, so $x lt.eq y$ almost surely.

#thm([Lemma 7.7.])[
  (Marginals and conditional crossings).  #emph[Put
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.33)")
 $ H^2 = exp (20 (2 sum_(s gt.eq 1) kappa_s^2 + 1)) < oo . $ <p:eq:uniform-product-density>]

 For every $j gt.eq 1$, the law just constructed has the following properties.]
  + #emph[Every one-coordinate endpoint density is at most $10$.
    Both marginals of $nu_j$ have $L^2 \( mu \)$ densities of norm at most $H$.
    The same bound holds for the product law formed by any deterministic choice of the $x$-marginal or the
    $y$-marginal at each coordinate.]
  + #emph[Conditional on $x$, the coordinates $y_i$ are independent.
    Conditional on $y$, the coordinates $x_i$ are independent.
    If $i in J_s$, $w = a_s$ or $b_s$, and $u in \[ w \, 1 - w \]$, then
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.34)")
 $ bb(P)_j \( x_i < u lt.eq y_i divides x \) & gt.eq rho_s \( w \) \/ 10 &  & upright("on ") { x_i < u } \,\
bb(P)_j \( x_i < u lt.eq y_i divides y \) & gt.eq rho_s \( w \) \/ 10 &  & upright("on ") { y_i gt.eq
  u } . $ <p:eq:crossing-given-x>]

 The assertions hold almost surely, simultaneously for any specified countable family of such thresholds.]
] <p:lem:coupling-marginals>

#proof[
  For a top or bottom component of width $w$, each endpoint interval has length at least $w \/ 2$,
    so its density is at most $2 \/ w$. A tagged endpoint density is at most $m$.
    Consequently either one-coordinate marginal density $h_i$ satisfies
  $ 0 < q_(j \, s) lt.eq h_i lt.eq 1 + 4 sum_(w = a_s \, b_s) frac(rho_s \( w \), w) +
    upright(bold(1))_({ s = j }) lt.eq 2 + 8 kappa_s < 10 . $
  Throughout these sums the two widths are counted with their labels.
  If $v$ is the uniform density on an interval of length at least $w \/ 2$, then $integral \( v - 1 \) =
    0$ and $parallel v - 1 parallel_2^2 lt.eq 2 \/ w$. The corresponding bound for a tagged interval is $m$.
    Subtracting $1$ from $h_i$ expresses it as the sum of four weighted mean-zero perturbations and, when $s = j$,
    one tagged perturbation. Cauchy–Schwarz for these at most five summands gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.35)")
   $ parallel h_i - 1 parallel_2^2 & lt.eq 20 sum_(w = a_s \, b_s) frac(rho_s \( w \)^2, w) +
     5 upright(bold(1))_({ s = j }) / m_s\
 & lt.eq 20 (frac(2 kappa_s^2, m_s) + upright(bold(1))_({ s = j }) / m_s) . $ <p:eq:marginal-energy>]
  This estimate holds for either endpoint. Summing over the $m_s$ coordinates of every block gives the bound
    $20 \( 2 sum_s kappa_s^2 + 1 \)$ for every deterministic selection of endpoint marginals.
    Each selected coordinate density is nonnegative and has integral one, and the coordinate laws are independent.
    Thus Lemma 13 supplies the asserted $L^2$ density with norm at most $H$.
    In particular, for each such product law $eta$ and every Borel set $A subset.eq X$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.36)")
   $ eta \( A \) lt.eq H mu \( A \)^(1 \/ 2) . $ <p:eq:uniform-absolute-continuity>]
  To verify the assertions after conditioning on an entire endpoint, we describe the conditional kernels.
    At coordinate $i in J_s$, let $ell$ run through the replacement labels, including the tag when present.
    Write $pi_ell$ for its mixture probability and $u_ell \, v_ell$ for the normalized interval densities of its
    two endpoints. The marginal densities in this coordinate are
  $ h_i^x \( t \) = q_(j \, s) + sum_ell pi_ell u_ell \( t \) \, wide h_i^y \( t \) = q_(j \, s) +
    sum_ell pi_ell v_ell \( t \) . $
  They are strictly positive. A conditional law of $y_i$ given $x_i = t$ is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.37)")
   $ K_i^x \( t \, d z \) = frac(q_(j \, s), h_i^x \( t \)) delta_t \( d z \) +
     sum_ell frac(pi_ell u_ell \( t \), h_i^x \( t \)) v_ell \( z \) thin d z . $ <p:eq:conditional-kernel>]
  The reverse kernel $K_i^y$ interchanges $u_ell$ and $v_ell$.
    Independence of the coordinate pairs shows, first for cylinder tests,
    that $⨂_i K_i^x \( x_i \, dot.op \)$ is a conditional law of $y$ given $x$.
    The monotone class theorem extends the defining identity to all Borel tests on $X^2$.
    The corresponding product of reverse kernels is a conditional law of $x$ given $y$.
    These formulas prove both assertions of conditional independence.
  If $x_i = t < u lt.eq 1 - w$, then $t in \[ 0 \, 1 - w \/ 2 \)$,
    where the top component of width $w$ has $x_i$-density $\( 1 - w \/ 2 \)^(- 1) gt.eq 1$.
    Its whole $y_i$ interval lies above $u$. Since $h_i^x lt.eq 10$,
    its contribution to (7.37) is at least $rho_s \( w \) \/ 10$. This proves (7.34).
    If $y_i = t gt.eq u gt.eq w$, the bottom component has $y_i$-density $\( 1 - w \/ 2 \)^(- 1) gt.eq 1$ at $t$,
    and its whole $x_i$ interval lies below $u$. The reverse kernel therefore proves (7.34).
    Intersecting the full-measure sets for any specified countable family of thresholds gives the final assertion. ◻
]

It remains to establish the pair identity under these probability laws.
  We first replace a finite prefix of $x$ by coordinates of $y$.
  The resulting law is dominated by a finite sum of the triangular laws $lambda_I$,
  but its diagonal tail retains the biased $x$-distribution.
  Keeping that tail density explicit permits the transfer to infinitely many replacements.

#thm([Proposition 7.8.])[
  (The identity under the biased couplings).  #emph[Let $p in
    M$ be an orthogonal projection satisfying $\( 1 - p \) T p = 0$ in the product model. For every $j gt.eq 1$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.38)")
 $ \( 1 - p \( y \) \) "diag"_(k in bb(Z)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] R_(x \, y) \( k \)
   #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] p \( x \) = 0 wide upright("for ") nu_j upright("-almost every ") \( x \, y \) .
   $ <p:eq:biased-identity>]

 Moreover, $0 < R_(x \, y) \( k \) lt.eq 1$ for all $k in bb(Z)$ almost surely.]
] <p:prop:biased-identity>

#proof[
  Fix $j$. For $D gt.eq 1$, define
  $ y_i^(\( D \)) = cases(delim: "{", y_i \, & i lt.eq D \,, x_i \, & i > D \,) $
  and let $beta_D$ be the law of $\( x \, y^(\( D \)) \)$ under $bb(P)_j$. We claim that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.39)")
   $ beta_D lt.double sum_(I subset.eq { 1 \, dots.h \, D }) lambda_I . $ <p:eq:finite-prefix-domination>]
  Condition on a pattern $c$ of mixture labels in the first $D$ coordinates. There are finitely many patterns.
    Let $I \( c \)$ consist of the coordinates carrying replacement labels.
    At each $i in I \( c \)$ the conditioned pair has density
  $ r_(c \, i) \( t \, z \) =
    frac(upright(bold(1))_(U_(c \, i)) \( t \) upright(bold(1))_(V_(c \, i)) \( z \), \| U_(c \, i) \| thin \|
    V_(c \, i) \|) $
  relative to the unnormalized triangular measure $upright(bold(1))_({ 0 lt.eq t < z < 1 }) thin d t thin d z$.
    Here $U_(c \, i) \, V_(c \, i)$ are the two intervals of the selected component.
    Their lengths are positive and their rectangle is supported on $t < z$.
    The remaining prefix coordinates are uniformly diagonal.
  For the diagonal tail, let $g$ be the full $x$-marginal density from Lemma 29.
    Write $mu = mu_(lt.eq D) ⊗ mu_(> D)$ and define
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.40)")
   $ g_D \( z \) = integral g \( u \, z \) thin d mu_(lt.eq D) \( u \) \, wide
     parallel g_D parallel_(L^2 \( mu_(> D) \)) lt.eq parallel g parallel_(L^2 \( mu \)) lt.eq
     H . $ <p:eq:tail-density>]
  Fubini identifies $g_D$ as the density of $x_(> D)$, and Jensen’s inequality proves the norm bound.
    Independence across coordinates ensures that conditioning on $c$ leaves this tail law unchanged.
    Therefore the conditional law of $\( x \, y^(\( D \)) \)$ has density
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.41)")
   $ g_D \( x_(> D) \) product_(i in I \( c \)) r_(c \, i) \( x_i \, y_i \) $ <p:eq:conditioned-prefix-density>]
  with respect to $lambda_(I \( c \))$. This follows on cylinder sets from the coordinate construction,
    and hence on all Borel sets. The rectangle factors form a finite product and are bounded.
    The tail factor is integrable and need not equal one. Summing the conditional laws over the finitely many
    patterns proves (7.39).
  Proposition 28 now gives the pair identity $beta_D$-almost everywhere.
    To pass to the limit, the first marginal of $beta_D$ is the $x$-marginal of $nu_j$,
    while its second marginal uses the $y$-marginals in the first $D$ coordinates and the $x$-marginals
    thereafter. Lemma 29 and (7.36) therefore give uniform absolute continuity of both marginal families,
    and absolute continuity of the two marginals of $nu_j$.
  On the common probability space, $y^(\( D \)) arrow.r y$ in the product topology.
    Since $X^2$ is compact metrizable, bounded convergence against continuous functions proves $beta_D
    arrow.r.double nu_j$. Every one of these laws is carried by ordered pairs.
    Their absolutely continuous marginals put both endpoints in the common conull set on which the cocycles are
    finite and positive. The ordered-coordinate formula for the cocycle ratios consequently gives $0 <
    R_(x \, y^(\( D \))) \( k \) lt.eq 1$ and $0 < R_(x \, y) \( k \) lt.eq 1$,
    simultaneously for every signed $k$. All hypotheses of Lemma 11 are now satisfied, with the fixed cap $B = 1$.
    That lemma transfers the identity from $beta_D$ to $nu_j$, proving (7.38). ◻
]

=== 7.5 Verifying the convolution obstruction <p:sec:obstruction>

It remains to exclude invariant projections. We verify the hypotheses of Lemma 17 for the product couplings.
  The infinite-sum estimate is already contained in that lemma;
  the task here is to bound the finite coefficients on an event of fixed positive probability and to attenuate
  every omitted index.

The preceding sections have constructed the factor $M$, its bounded operator $T$, and all the couplings,
  with the scales and angles fixed independently of any projection.
  Let $p in M$ be an orthogonal projection satisfying $\( 1 - p \) T p = 0$, and suppose, toward a contradiction,
  that $0 < p < 1$. Use the common invariant Borel conull set for this fixed $p$ and the cocycles. Equation (7.29) gives

$ 0 < r \( x \) = p \( x \)_(0 \, 0) < 1 wide upright("for ") mu upright("-almost every ") x . $

Every coupling used below has absolutely continuous endpoint marginals,
  so its endpoints belong to this set almost surely. All pointwise operator and cocycle statements below are
  understood on these sets. Only countably many indices occur, so they may be required simultaneously.

===== 7.5.1 Convolution identities. <convolution-identities.>

Set

$ xi \( x \) = p \( x \) e_0 \, wide gamma \( y \) = \( 1 - p \( y \) \) e_0 . $

These measurable vectors have norms at most one, with $xi_0 \( x \) = r \( x \)$ and $gamma_0 \( y \) = 1 -
  r \( y \)$. Fix $j$. By Proposition 30, the vector

$ zeta = "diag"_(v in bb(Z)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] R_(x \, y) \( v \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] xi \( x \) $

belongs to $"ran" p \( y \)$ for $nu_j$-almost every $\( x \, y \)$.
  The same range is invariant under the fiber $T \( y \)$ and therefore under all its nonnegative powers.
  By the cocycle identity, the coordinate $n + v$ of $T \( y \)^n zeta$ is

$ F_n \( sigma^v y \) thin R_(x \, y) \( v \) thin xi_v \( x \) =
  frac(F_(n + v) \( y \), F_v \( x \)) xi_v \( x \) \, wide n gt.eq 0 . $

Taking its inner product with $gamma \( y \)$ yields

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.42)")
 $ 0 = sum_(v in bb(Z)) frac(F_(n + v) \( y \), F_v \( x \)) xi_v \( x \) overline(gamma_(n + v) \( y \)) =
   sum_(v in bb(Z)) A_v B_(- n - v) \, wide n gt.eq 0 \, $ <p:eq:convolution-identity>]

where

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.43)")
 $ A_v = frac(xi_v \( x \), F_v \( x \)) \, wide B_v = F_(- v) \( y \) overline(gamma_(- v) \( y \)) .
   $ <p:eq:coefficient-sequences>]

These sequences themselves need not be square summable. Every convolution in (7.42), however,
  is absolutely convergent. Indeed $F_(k + 1) \( z \) = f \( sigma^k z \) F_k \( z \) lt.eq
  F_k \( z \)$ for every integer $k$, so for $n gt.eq 0$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.44)")
 $ 0 < frac(F_(n + v) \( y \), F_v \( x \)) lt.eq min { R_(x \, y) \( v \) \, R_(x \, y) \( n + v \) } lt.eq
   1 . $ <p:eq:two-ratio-bounds>]

The first bound uses $F_(n + v) \( y \) lt.eq F_v \( y \)$ and the second uses $F_v \( x \) gt.eq
  F_(n + v) \( x \)$. Cauchy–Schwarz now bounds the absolute sum by $parallel xi \( x \) parallel_2 parallel
  gamma \( y \) parallel_2 lt.eq 1$.

===== 7.5.2 An event of uniformly positive probability. <an-event-of-uniformly-positive-probability.>

Write $K = K_j$ and $m = m_j$. On the augmented probability space $\( Omega_j \, bb(P)_j \)$,
  define $E_j$ by the following conditions:

+ For every $i in J_j$, $x_i < 1 - 2 \/ m$ and $y_i > 2 \/ m$,
  and at least one coordinate in $J_j$ has the tagged label.
+ For every $s > j$ and $i in J_s$, $x_i < 1 - 2 K \/ N_s$ and $y_i > 2 K \/ N_s$.
+ $r \( x \) gt.eq 1 \/ j$ and $1 - r \( y \) gt.eq 1 \/ j$.

We claim that there is a constant $c_(*) > 0$ such that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.45)")
 $ bb(P)_j \( E_j \) gt.eq c_(*) wide upright("for all sufficiently large ") j . $ <p:eq:positive-event>]

The constant can be chosen independently of $p$; the point beyond which the bound holds may depend on $p$.

At one coordinate of $J_j$, a tagged choice has probability $1 \/
  m$ and satisfies both of the first condition’s inequalities.
  By the one-coordinate density bound in Lemma 29, the probability of a nontagged choice satisfying those
  inequalities is at least

$ 1 - 1 / m - 10 2 / m - 10 2 / m = 1 - 41 / m . $

The disjoint events specifying exactly one tag and admissible nontagged choices at the other coordinates
  consequently give the lower bound

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.46)")
 $ m 1 / m (1 - 41 / m)^(m - 1) = (1 - 41 / m)^(m - 1) gt.eq e^(- 82) . $ <p:eq:tag-event>]

Here $m gt.eq 100$ and $log \( 1 - t \) gt.eq - 2 t$ for $0 lt.eq t lt.eq 1 \/ 2$.
  The probability that the second condition fails is at most

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.47)")
 $ 40 K sum_(s > j) m_s / N_s = 40 K sum_(s > j) 1 / K_s lt.eq frac(80 K_j, K_(j + 1)) arrow.r
   0 . $ <p:eq:future-event-loss>]

This is a countable union bound with a convergent sum. The intervals involved have length $2 K \/ N_s$;
  all are less than one by the scale conditions.

The two endpoint losses in the third condition are bounded, using (7.36), by

$ bb(P)_j { r \( x \) < 1 \/ j } lt.eq H mu { r < 1 \/ j }^(1 \/ 2) \, wide bb(P)_j { 1 -
  r \( y \) < 1 \/ j } lt.eq H mu { 1 - r < 1 \/ j }^(1 \/ 2) . $

Both tend to zero because $0 < r < 1$ almost everywhere. Subtracting these losses and (7.47) from (7.46) proves
  (7.45), for example with $c_(*) = e^(- 82) \/ 2$. No independence between these conditions is needed.

===== 7.5.3 The finite coefficients. <the-finite-coefficients.>

On $E_j$ the coefficients in (7.43) satisfy

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.48)")
 $ \| A_v \| \, \| B_v \| lt.eq D_j quad \( \| v \| lt.eq K \) \, wide \| A_0 \| \, \| B_0 \| gt.eq
   1 \/ j . $ <p:eq:middle-coefficients>]

To verify all signs, first let $0 lt.eq v lt.eq K$. For $i in J_j$, $v theta_i lt.eq 2 \/ m$,
  and for $i in J_s$ with $s > j$, $v theta_i lt.eq 2 K \/ N_s$.
  The defining endpoint exclusions in $E_j$ therefore give

$ floor.l x_i + v theta_i floor.r = 0 \, wide floor.l y_i - v theta_i floor.r =
  0 quad \( i in J_s \, med s gt.eq j \) . $

At each of the finitely many earlier coordinates, these floors have absolute value at most $K + 1$,
  since $0 < theta_i < 1$. Thus

$ frac(1, F_v \( x \)) lt.eq exp (\( K + 1 \) sum_(s < j) C_s m_s) = D_j \, wide F_(- v) \( y \) lt.eq D_j \, $

which bounds $A_v$ and $B_v$. For the negative coefficient indices, monotonicity gives $F_(- v) \( x \) gt.eq
  1$ and $F_v \( y \) lt.eq 1$, and hence

$ \| A_(- v) \| lt.eq 1 \, wide \| B_(- v) \| lt.eq 1 wide \( 0 lt.eq v lt.eq K \) . $

Finally $A_0 = r \( x \)$ and $B_0 = 1 - r \( y \)$, proving (7.48).

Thus the finite-coefficient hypotheses of Lemma 17 hold with $D = D_j$ and $a = 1 \/ j$.
  We next verify its attenuation hypothesis outside this window.

===== 7.5.4 Attenuation at every omitted index. <attenuation-at-every-omitted-index.>

We next control $R_(x \, y) \( k \)$ when $\| k \| > K$. If $K < \| k \| lt.eq N_j \/ 4$,
  choose a tagged coordinate in $J_j$ on $E_j$. At that coordinate

$ 1 / m < \| k \| theta_i lt.eq 1 / 2 \, wide x_i < 1 / m \, wide y_i gt.eq 1 - 1 / m . $

For $k > 0$ the threshold $1 - { k theta_i }$ lies in $\[ 1 \/ 2 \, 1 - 1 \/ m \)$;
  for $k < 0$ it equals $\| k \| theta_i$ and lies in $\( 1 \/ m \, 1 \/ 2 \]$.
  The tagged pair crosses it in both cases. The coordinate contribution to the ratio gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.49)")
 $ R_(x \, y) \( k \) lt.eq e^(- C_j) lt.eq e^(- L_j) quad upright("on ") E_j \, wide K < \| k \| lt.eq
   N_j \/ 4 . $ <p:eq:tag-attenuation>]

Fix $j$ sufficiently large that the phase conclusions of Proposition 24 hold in every block $s gt.eq j$.
  For $\| k \| > N_j \/ 4$ we can assign a block $J_s$ and a width $w$ so that either the second phase range is
  used with $s gt.eq j$, $w = b_s$, or the first phase range is used with $s > j$, $w = a_s$.
  Here is the coverage explicitly. Put

$ alpha_s = G_s K_s \, wide beta_s = N_s \/ 4 \, wide Q_s = m_s^(\( 4 \/ 5 \) m_s) . $

The scale conditions imply

$ alpha_s lt.eq beta_s < Q_s \, wide alpha_(s + 1) < Q_s \, wide Q_s arrow.r oo . $

Start with $\[ beta_j \, Q_j \]$, where the second phase range applies.
  For each $s > j$, the first and second phase ranges together cover $\[ alpha_s \, Q_s \]$, divided at $beta_s$.
  Consecutive intervals overlap by $alpha_(s + 1) < Q_s$, so their union with the initial interval covers
  $\[ beta_j \, oo \)$. These are real intervals; in particular every integer magnitude in question is covered.

Choose one such assignment deterministically for each $k$,
  and let $I \( k \) subset.eq J_s$ be a deterministic list of qualifying coordinates.
  The choice can, for example, use the least available block and then increasing coordinate order.
  With $q = \| I \( k \) \|$ we have

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.50)")
 $ q gt.eq m_s \/ 20 \, wide u_i := 1 - { k theta_i } in \[ w \, 1 - w \] quad \( i in I \( k \) \) .
   $ <p:eq:tail-thresholds>]

All choices depend only on $j \, k$ and the already fixed parameters and angles, not on the sampled endpoints.
  In the second phase range,

$ m_s rho_s \( b_s \) = kappa_s m_s^(1 \/ 40) gt.eq L_s gt.eq L_j \; $

in the first phase range, where $s > j$,

$ m_s rho_s \( a_s \) = kappa_s sqrt(G_s) gt.eq L_(s - 1) gt.eq L_j . $

Also $C_s gt.eq L_s gt.eq L_j$ in either case. If any listed threshold is crossed, then

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.51)")
 $ R_(x \, y) \( k \) lt.eq e^(- C_s) lt.eq e^(- L_j) . $ <p:eq:tail-crossing-attenuation>]

Let $cal(B)_k$ be the event that none of the thresholds in (7.50) is crossed, and define

$ a_k \( x \) = \# { i in I \( k \) : x_i < u_i } \, $

$ H_k^(\( 1 \)) & = cal(B)_k ∩ { a_k \( x \) gt.eq q \/ 2 } \,\
H_k^(\( 2 \)) & = cal(B)_k ∩ { a_k \( x \) < q \/ 2 } . $

Given $x$, the eligible coordinates in $H_k^(\( 1 \))$ are fixed. Conditional independence and (7.34) imply

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.52)")
 $ bb(P)_j \( H_k^(\( 1 \)) divides x \) lt.eq \( 1 - rho_s \( w \) \/ 10 \)^(q \/ 2) lt.eq
   exp \( - m_s rho_s \( w \) \/ 400 \) lt.eq e^(- L_j \/ 400) . $ <p:eq:first-bad-probability>]

For the other orientation, orderedness gives on $cal(B)_k$ the exact equivalence

$ x_i < u_i quad arrow.l.r.double quad y_i < u_i wide \( i in I \( k \) \) . $

Indeed the forward implication follows from the absence of a crossing, and the reverse implication from $x_i lt.eq
  y_i$. Thus

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.53)")
 $ H_k^(\( 2 \)) = cal(B)_k ∩ {\# { i in I \( k \) : y_i gt.eq u_i } > q \/ 2} quad
   upright("almost surely") . $ <p:eq:second-bad-event>]

This is the same complementary conditioning split shown in Figure 1,
  with independent coordinates in place of digit labels. The eligible coordinates in this expression are
  determined by $y$. The reverse conditional independence and (7.34) give

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.54)")
 $ bb(P)_j \( H_k^(\( 2 \)) divides y \) lt.eq e^(- L_j \/ 400) . $ <p:eq:second-bad-probability>]

These estimates hold under the full probability law $bb(P)_j$, before any restriction to $E_j$.
  There are countably many $k$, so the conditional estimates can be taken simultaneously outside endpoint null
  sets. No union estimate over their bad events has been made.

For the near-window indices in (7.49), set both exceptional events equal to the empty set.
  Combining that estimate with (7.51) and the two conditional bounds, we have, for every $\| k \| > K_j$,

$ R_(x \, y) \( k \) lt.eq e^(- L_j) + upright(bold(1))_(H_k^(\( 1 \))) + upright(bold(1))_(H_k^(\( 2 \))) quad
  upright("on ") E_j \, $

where

$ bb(P)_j \( H_k^(\( 1 \)) divides x \) lt.eq e^(- L_j \/ 400) \, wide bb(P)_j \( H_k^(\( 2 \)) divides y \) lt.eq
  e^(- L_j \/ 400) $

hold under the full law. All inputs to Lemma 17 are now verified:
  the vectors, convolutions and ratio bounds are those of (7.42)–(7.44);
  the event and finite coefficient bounds are (7.45) and (7.48).
  For every sufficiently large fixed $j$, the lemma therefore gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.55)")
 $ c_(*) epsilon_j & lt.eq \( 2 K_j + 1 \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] e^(- L_j) +
   2 e^(- L_j \/ 800) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]\
 & lt.eq \( 2 K_j + 1 \) 3 e^(- L_j \/ 10000) lt.eq epsilon_j / j \, $ <p:eq:final-contradiction>]

by (7.2). Since $epsilon_j > 0$, cancellation at this fixed stage gives $c_(*) lt.eq 1 \/ j$,
  impossible once $j > 1 \/ c_(*)$. No uniform lower bound on $\( epsilon_j \)$ is used.
  The scales, angles, operator and coupling laws were fixed before the arbitrary projection $p$;
  only its conull representatives and the eventual threshold in (7.45) depended on $p$.
  This excludes every nontrivial invariant projection in $M$.

=== 7.6 Finite atomic algebras and a diffuse limit <p:sec:structure>

The model also distinguishes the spectral information in finitely many polar conjugates from that in the full
  orbit. This distinction follows from the explicit weight and the rotation, independently of the convolution argument.

#thm([Proposition 7.9.])[
  #emph[For the operator $T$ constructed above, its polar decomposition is $T = U \| T \|$, with $\| T \| =
    pi \( f \)$ and $ker \| T \| = { 0 }$. The spectral measure of $\| T \|$ is countably atomic.
    For every finite $F subset bb(Z)$, the abelian von Neumann algebra
$ cal(A)_F = W^(*) \( U^n \| T \| U^(- n) : n in F \) $

 is atomic. The full algebra
$ cal(A) = W^(*) \( U^n \| T \| U^(- n) : n in bb(Z) \) $

 is diffuse.]
] <p:prop:joint-structure>

#proof[
  Since $f > 0$ almost everywhere, $T^(*) T = pi \( f \)^2$ has zero kernel, and $\| T \| = pi \( f \)$.
    The unitary $U$ is therefore its polar factor. On the conull set where the sum in (7.23) has finite support,
    the value of $f$ is determined by a finite subset of the countable coordinate set.
    There are only countably many such subsets. Consequently $f$ takes values in a countable subset of
    $\( 0 \, 1 \]$, up to a null set. Its nonnull level sets give countably many spectral projections whose sum is
    $1$, which proves the assertion about spectral type.
  The representation convention gives
  $ U pi \( g \) U^(*) = pi \( g compose sigma^(- 1) \) . $
  For a finite $F$, the tuple $\( f compose sigma^(- n) \)_(n in F)$ has countable range on a common conull set.
    The nonnull joint level sets therefore give an atomic partition of the generated algebra $cal(A)_F$.
    This includes $F = diameter$, when the generated unital algebra is scalar.
  For the infinite family, $cal(A)$ is an invariant abelian subalgebra of $pi \( L^oo \( X \) \)$.
    The rotation is totally ergodic. Indeed, for any integer $q eq.not
    0$ and any nonconstant finite-support character indexed by $v = \( v_i \)$,
    rational independence implies $q sum_i v_i theta_i in.not bb(Z)$.
    The Fourier argument used in Section 7.1 thus proves ergodicity of $sigma^q$.
  Suppose $e$ were an atom of $cal(A)$. Its integer translates under $"Ad" U$ are atoms of equal positive trace.
    Distinct atoms in an abelian algebra are orthogonal, so only finitely many can occur.
    The orbit of $e$ is therefore finite, and for some positive integer $q$, $U^q e U^(- q) = e$.
    Because $e in pi \( L^oo \( X \) \)$, total ergodicity forces $e = 1$.
    An abelian algebra whose identity is an atom is scalar. But $cal(A)$ contains the nonconstant multiplication
    operator $pi \( f \)$: a positive almost-everywhere constant would have finite logarithmic integral,
    contrary to Lemma 27. Hence $cal(A)$ has no atoms, as claimed. ◻
]

The factor construction, the norm and determinant calculation, the exclusion of invariant projections,
  and Proposition 31 prove Theorem 23, with $c_i = C_j$ for $i in J_j$.
  The diffuseness statement is only about $cal(A)$; it does not identify $cal(A)$ with all of $pi \( L^oo \( X \) \)$.

